How Do We Use Our Gifts for God?
You can read the manuscript below:
Have you ever heard kids ask questions?
That magical question “why” can go on for hours and hours.
Well it seems that there’s a little child in my soul that likes to ask why, but it’s not about abstract questions, it’s about everyday things.
There are some people who are naturally driven, and the fact that something needs to get done is enough motivation for them.
In order for me to get anything done, I need the sense that I’m doing something grand.
Unfortunately, details and tedious tasks are the majority of real life.
2 years later, “Between Two Worlds” dropped and I wanted to make sense of what it means to live life in a fallen world while waiting for a Hero to save the day.
We don’t have to be robots.
I want to challenge the lies we’ve been told, and present a new and more glorious picture.
The discussion was about campus ministry, the local church, the Great Commission, etc. :
This is probably why I struggled so much in high school, even after I became a Christian.
I know I’m not the only one that can finds it difficult to be motivated sometimes.
We sometimes only want to be motivated by big things, but everything is big because everything is part of the big story.
This is even true of our gifts.
Like all of life is a little resumé that leads to others being impressed with us.
Your gifts are there to help tell the big story.
I remember when I began to think of everything this way.
15 He is the image of the invisible God, the firstborn of all creation.
He doesn’t leave anything out.
Later in the book (and elsewhere) Paul refers to evil spirits as rulers or authorities.
Well if even demons were created by Jesus, for Jesus, then surely our gifts are.
Presidents are elected.
God alone is He who’s authority has always existed.
He was never elected, and he’ll never be impeached.
Therefore, God doesn’t intend to get glory just from part of your life, but from all of it.
When I buy furniture from IKEA, I already know two things: 1, this will probably fall apart next year.
Wouldn’t it be dumb if I took some of those screws that were meant for a bookshelf and used them for something else?
The gifts that every single person possesses are tools in your storytelling toolbox.
What about my personal fulfillment?
Well the greatest role you can ever play, is a role in the big picture.
But how do we get to a place where we use our gifts for God’s glory?
And it’s because we don’t see God as glorious enough.
When you read Isaiah 40 you see that attempting to take his glory for yourself or someone else is nothing less than robbery.
I’d encourage you to read those parts of Scripture where people brag about God.
Hannah’s prayer in 2 Samuel 2.
Or even in the arts.
Listen to what he says in Colossians 3.
You are serving the Lord Christ.
Paul isn’t under the impression that administrative gifts, or gifts to help children, or gifts just to do a mundane job well can’t be used for the glory of Jesus.
We can be tempted to work lazily.
But Paul says work heartily or with all of your heart.
Sometimes we can be tempted to work hard, but to do it for the wrong people.
A paycheck isn’t enough to build your life around, but eternal rewards are.
Have you ever noticed how we scheme for the things we’re truly wrapped up in?
And some times I can get too wrapped up in it.
And then it looked like it wasn’t going to work out.
We seem to scheme for the things that we love.
And when you love the glory of God you find ways to use every nook and cranny of your life for it.
My advice is this: Chase Jesus and fall in love with him until you see everything as an opportunity to tell people about Him.
This was much needed and taken to heart.
Can’t find a better way of how that could be said.
Thats why it’s so important to continue reading the scriptures diligently, the Bible always renews our mind.
It’s important to put into practice what we learn from scripture and make it a habitual practice.
God’s way is always the best way, Christlikeness is the way of peace and joy and glorifying God.
