My new book, Rise, comes out January 27th!
And so, most of us spend our youth in a sad state of slumber-sleeping in on life until we’re forced to get up.
It’s happening right now.
In this powerful book, Trip Lee argues it’s time to wake up and RISE, to live the way we were created to live.
You’ll probably rush over there and if you get one in time, that bite is a lot sweeter.
But we should see them as precious.
Maybe we think only what we write down, or only when we have groundbreaking conversations or only when we say what we really think our words matter.
Jesus says these words in the midst of a conversation with the Pharisees, after they accuse Him of casting out demons by Satan.
He tells them that all of our words are the fruit of what’s taken root in our hearts.
It’s true for every single one of us.
He says, “For by your words you will be acquitted, and by your words you will be condemned.” But why?
Why will we be acquitted or condemned based on something as trivial as the words we say?
He makes that clear earlier when He says, “Out of the overflow of the heart the mouth speaks.” Have you ever felt unfairly judged?
Well being judged by our words isn’t like that.
We can be accurately judged by our words because they are an accurate reflection of what’s happening in our hearts.
I could tell you water is in there, or juice is in there.
It’s the same with our words and our hearts.
Our words always show what’s in our hearts, whether good or evil.
This happened to President Obama when he was talking to the President of Russia.
They didn’t expect to be held accountable for those words, but they were.
We think we say things, and itʼs done, but they will come back to bite us.
There is a day – the text calls it the Day of Judgment – when we will stand before the holy Judge of the entire universe.
The evidence will be presented.
God will examine the way we spoke to strangers on the street.
God will comb through our angry rants in traffic on the drive to work.
Even the words we send in text messages or emails.
You notice Jesus doesn’t say we will have to give an account for the important words we speak.
Every word, no matter how insignificant you think it is.
And here’s the thing.
Which of course means, all of us should be condemned for our words.
Those three words are “It.
Finished.” After having lived the perfect life, after having only used his words in a way that glorified God, Jesus went to the cross.
And He rose three days later.
And those words will testify in our favor.
And praise God that the blood of the Lord Jesus covers those words.
So as believers, though we won’t be condemned for our words, we’ll still be held accountable for them.
We make budgets, we balance our checkbooks, we keep our receipts, and we look at our online billing statements.
We know we have to steward our money well.
We should be good stewards of the words we use.
So I want to give three ways that we can use our words well.
Jesus has given us access to the Father, and we should come boldly before His throne often.
He always hears our prayers.
Often when we’re going through something difficult, the first thing we do is just vent to friends of family.
And before agreeing or offering any counsel, she just asked me simply, “Have you been praying about it?” And the answer was no.
That would have been a far better use of my words.
It’s a waste of words sometimes.
There’s no better use of your words.
So when we need help, instead of just being mad, we should just talk to the One who’s in charge.
We should remember that talking to God about the situation is more important and effective than talking to the other person.
Ask Him for help and grace and wisdom.
We should just praise Him some times.
More than just, “He is good.
He is worthy of our praise.
God has spoken in these last days through His Son Jesus Christ.
Romans 10 tells us that, “Faith comes through hearing and hearing through the Word of Christ.” This is how God saves men and women.
I want you to think for a moment about what person in your life needs to hear the Gospel.
What person in your life needs to hear the Gospel?
It’s good for them to know you care about them.
It would be devastating if we had 10 conversations about politics, but not one about the Gospel.
And I’m talking about myself.
And whenever that happens, it reminds me, that some times it’s good to wait, but sometimes I need to tell folks about Jesus right now.
And by all means, follow the leading of the Spirit, but know that He never leads us to put off stuff that needs to be said now.
This doesn’t mean that should be our only interaction with non-Christians.
Maybe we should bring them to church and use the sermon as a launching to pad to talk about it.
Find a way to tell the Gospel.
Build Up Others (G- Grace)
Only what is helpful for building others up.
Jesus tells us that man does not live off bread alone, but every word that proceeds from the mouth of God.
And we should be echoing those precious words to each other every chance we get.
I think this is something CHBC does exceptionally well.
“Did they just encourage me about the question I asked?” Up under this encouragement is affirmation.
Tell them when you see evidences of God’s grace in their lives.
We shouldn’t think “build up” means the same as cute, inspirational sounding words.
Sometimes it looks like challenging a brother or sister and calling them to repent of a sin.
Encourage your brothers and sisters in the Gospel.
And also challenge your brothers and sisters to fight the good fight of faith, to persevere in trusting Jesus.
Another way you can build others up is by giving good counsel.
Be available to hear about what’s going on in your brother or sister’s life.
On the other hand, we shouldn’t be afraid to talk about things other than Jesus.
Some of you need to stop talking so much and others need to start talking more.
Let all your words be characterized by love.
As you seek to apply what you’ve heard tonight, don’t make the mistake of just trying to keep track of what you say and just trying to do better.
As we see earlier in the text, what’s stored up in our hearts comes out.
So store up good things in your heart and watch them overflow.
The real way to watch your words is to watch your hearts because they are so closely connected.
Watch your heart, and by doing that, watch your Words.
