Inspired By the Lowercase King — TRIP LEE - OFFICIAL SITE
In the Bible, he saw God’s vision for His world, He saw the prophets call for the end of oppression, and He was passionate to see that justice come to pass.
I want to fight for the oppressed and preach Good News to the hopeless.
He didn’t sit back and complain, he stepped up and led.
This resulted in the non-violent resistance that helped change the United States forever.
Vision is only valuable when it’s married to leadership.
Of course, his fight was not met with smiles and embraces.
He knew what he was doing was dangerous, yet he had the courage to put his life on the line for his people.
It only reminded him why he was fighting.
I hope you’re inspired by his example as well, and I hope you’ll join me in praising God for what He accomplished through Dr. King.
They ask him why he brought them in the desert to die.
And this is the kind of grumbling and complaining against God that characterized this generation.
Listen again to the text.
But I don’t think there should be any surprise here that their sin made God angry.
Growing up I had a good father, who was loving and attentive and we had a pretty good relationship.
So for a kid who wanted to get stuff and go places, I kind of felt like I had to play the game.
But my methods weren’t perfect.
He was unpredictable in that way.
It provokes His anger.
Because of sin He opened up the ground and dropped people through it down to Sheol in Numbers 15.
It communicates this picture of disgust.
So to answer our question, God does get angry and He responded to the sin of the previous generation with a furious, holy, disgust.
So is there a problem here?
If I consistently responded to my wife with anger and yelled at her, that would be a horrible sin on my part.
It’s not a character flaw.
And you shouldn’t feel the need to apologize to your non-Christian friends about God’s anger either.
It’s the way a holy God responds to sin.
But God as a perfectly holy God can only be angry in a righteous way.
And every sinner will have to answer to an angry God, unless somehow God’s anger aimed at them can be taken care of.
I know before I was a Christian, and even sometimes after that, I didn’t think of sin as a big deal.
It is an offense against a person – the most faithful person.
Maybe you think God is overreacting.
Why does it make Him so mad?
He says, “They are a people whose hearts go astray, and they have not known my ways.”
They don’t just go astray externally in their actions.
I happen to be horrible – I mean absolutely awful – with directions.
And it’s not because her directions were bad, it’s because I make wrong turns and go the wrong way and get turned around.
Their hearts travel down the wrong path and lead them to the wrong place.
And the same can be said of all sin.
And our actions follow.
And He gave them commands to follow His ways, to follow His path.
He’s the one who delivered them from Pharaoh and split the Red Sea!
They saw His works, but they didn’t know His ways.
That you’re not just falling off the horse or coming short of your full potential?
You’re saying, “God, I’ve seen your ways and I don’t like them.
I am wiser than you and a better Lord than you.
And this is what we do every time we sin.
We have to think rightly of our sin, or we won’t hate it like we should.
It’s ugly.
We may want to even change the way we talk about it.
Or oh, I’m struggling with this.” No we chose to disobey God.
That is the ugly reality of sin.
Ralph Venning helps us understand it in Sinfulness of Sin when he says, “In short, sin is the dare of God’s justice, the rape of his mercy, the jeer of his patience, the slight of his power, the contempt of his love.
The warning the Psalmist gives here begins with the words, “do not harden your heart.” Basically meaning don’t become increasingly stubborn and reject God’s commands.
Really hard hearts usually happens over long spans of time, not overnight.
Really hard hearts happen over time when we choose to disobey God in the little things over and over and over again.
And before you know it, we’re just pretending, until we get tired of pretending and stop following Jesus altogether.
Instead of trusting your ways, know His.
Trust Jesus and believe His Word.
Follow His path.
But by the time the Psalmist writes this, God’s people have already entered.
And this is how the writer of Hebrews uses it too.
We see here that not only does God get angry, but He makes decisions in His anger.
He does exactly what one of the earlier quotes says not to do.
This isn’t to say that all human anger is sinful.
Anger doesn’t intoxicate Him and move Him to make foolish decisions.
God’s eternal rest is the beautiful, peaceful, non-stressful, God glorifying place that all of His true people will end up in.
And we’re not talking about the wrath of an army or a man, but the wrath of almighty God on your head for an eternity.
He still hates sin today.
Their sin kept them from God’s rest, and ours can too.
After you die, you will either enter God’s rest or endure God’s wrath.
Because all of us are like the sinful generation he talks about.
We go astray in our hearts and we don’t follow His ways.
So is there hope for any of us?
In 1 Thessalonians 1:10, Paul describes a man from Galilee with these words, “his Son from heaven, whom he raised from the dead, Jesus who delivers us from the wrath to come.”
Our only hope is Jesus.
But for those of us in Christ, we don’t have to endure that wrath.
For those of us in Christ, He took the wrath that we stored up, poured it all in one cup, put it over the head of His Son and He poured all out on Him.
Jesus endured the terrifying wrath of God for sinners on the cross.
Blessed is the man whose sins are not counted against Him.
And we can sing, “Even so it is well with my soul.”
