The Man Who Has God For His Treasure — TRIP LEE - OFFICIAL SITE
The man who has God for his treasure has all things in One.
Or if he must see them go, one after one, he will scarcely feel a sense of loss, for having the Source of all things he has in One all satisfaction, all pleasure, all delight.
What we experienced We got to spend time with believers from a different country, with a different culture, from different backgrounds who love the same Jesus we do.
We also learned that Aussies talk weird.
They think we talk too though… All the dudes were rocking skinny jeans and they drive a lot of miniature cars.
Why I Loved It
The fellowship was rich, relationships were deepened, and I was edified by godly conversations.
Until next time, we love you Aussie!
Some have used hip hop to express their sincere religious belief or lack thereof.
I think hip hop is uniquely suited to express loyalty and devotion to anything we’re passionate about.
But most of the time hip hop is more like a barbershop than a Sunday School class.
And it often moves me to pray for my fellow emcees.
Rappers seem to say whatever words feel right in the moment – whether they actually believe them or not.
Of course, there are some things – like hypocrisy and money hungry “preachers” – that deserve to be mocked.
I admit, hearing rappers talk about “freaky” girls in the church choir makes me cringe, but nothing grieves me more than mocking Jesus Himself.
We would all be afraid for a man who’d spit in the face of the President, but we should be even more afraid for a man who spits in the face of Almighty God.
So how should I respond?
Or, as a rapper myself, I could write a scathing diss record for the ages.
But honestly, I don’t think they would do much to address the real problem.
Our words are always a perfect picture of what’s going on inside of us.
Whatever you have in the glass spills out onto the ground.
It’s the same thing with our words.
Speech and music doesn’t make us sinful, but it does expose what’s in our hearts and minds (Matthew 12:34).
First, they don’t understand who Jesus is.
Of course, they know Jesus was a Jewish man who was crucified two thousand years ago.
They wouldn’t toss his name around like He’s a nobody and treat His name with such levity and disrespect.
When you truly know the King, you submit to His authority, you don’t challenge it.
Second, we need the people who do know Jesus to speak up.
Reckless art is just one expression of it.
He’s real.
In fact, His Son died and took the punishment for sin so we wouldn’t have to.
Pass this Good News on to others!
Stop yelling at people, and start loving them.
It’s because God opened your blind eyes.
And if you don’t know Jesus, you should get to know Him.
There’s not a single one of us that can afford to ignore God or treat His Son like just another dude.
And the truth is, whether we believe in Him or not, we will be judged by Him.
Creator, Sustainer, and Judge.
