The detailed account reminded me why many people’s obsession with him borders on hero worship.
I want to begin by thinking about stories.
We have love stories, comedies, and tragedies – or the mixture of all those in one known as romantic comedy.
Is Jesus just an agent of good that we hope wins?
Well in the Gospel of Mark we get lots of opportunities to see Jesus interact with the forces of evil face to face.
And I would contend that there is a battle going on between Jesus and the forces of evil, but it’s not a fair fight.
Around this time in the Gospel of Mark, Jesus had been teaching and large crowds began to gather and listen to his teaching.
This is the story where the windstorm comes, the disciples panic, and Jesus calms the wind with His words, “Peace, be still.” So they were met with extreme opposition in the seas and Jesus took care of it.
Let’s read the text, starting at verse 1.
And when Jesus had stepped out of the boat, immediately there met him out of the tombs a man with an unclean spirit.
And no one could bind him anymore, not even with a chain, for he had often been bound with shackles and chains, but he wrenched the chains apart, and he broke the shackles in pieces. No one… had the strength… to subdue him.
And when he saw Jesus from afar…, he ran and fell down before him.
And the unclean spirits came out, and entered the pigs, and the herd, numbering about two thousand, rushed down the steep bank into the sea and were drowned in the sea.
And people came to see what it was that had happened.
And those who had seen it described to them what had happened to the demon-possessed man and to the pigs.
As he was getting into the boat, the man who had been possessed with demons begged him that he might be with him.
So as soon as they step out of the boat they’re met by this man with an unclean spirit.
This man who met them was possessed by a demon.
He lived among the dead.
He had superhuman strength, so much so that no chains could restrain him.
He was always screaming and crying out.
This man is disturbed.
One, because many of us have stopped believing in the supernatural.
But I want you to use your imagination with me for a minute.
You’re driving home one day and see a brother living in the graveyard, screaming in pain and torment.
I’d be calling some kind of authority to snatch him up.
He’s a danger to himself and to others and he’s not in his right mind.
Well the people in the country of the Gerasenes didn’t know what to do with him.
I’m sure they were scared our of their minds.
With voices in their heads, and violent tendencies, who cut themselves.
And similar to the towns people in the story, we don’t know what to do with them other than try to subdue them and keep them from hurting themselves and others.
Evil spiritual forces have taken over his body and are torturing him!
Demons are real.
He has a very similar incident in chapter 1.
Demons seek to undo and hinder God’s purposes in the world.
Demons’ work doesn’t only look like this kind of possession and torment though.
Still they are at work attacking us.
They spread false teaching.
Demons are responsible for much of the evil in the world.
And they are the enemies of the living God.
Back to the man.
No one has been able to deliver him.
But when he sees Jesus off in the distance he runs to Him and falls down before Him.
He sees Jesus, and knows that maybe Jesus can do something about these demons that torment him.
He sees the possibility of relief and runs at the chance.
He didn’t know how anyone could deliver him, but somehow he knew who could deliver him.
We need to stop looking to our will power, to our money, to our friends and we need to run to Jesus!
It’s similar when dealing with folks who are mentally unstable.
We should have compassion on them.
We should serve them holistically, use medication if needed, but point them to Jesus for deliverance.
They need an encounter with the Lord.
Let me draw your attention to another part of the scene.
I adjure you by God, do not torment me!” The text says he cries that out because Jesus was saying, “Come out of him you unclean spirit.” This shows us that this is the unclean spirit inside of him speaking.
Jesus is the Son of God and demons are against God’s purposes.
This would be like during a war.
Man what you doing here?
Please don’t hurt us!” Wouldn’t that be crazy?
Shouldn’t you be fighting instead of begging?” But this reveals that this isn’t a normal war.
So we are going to look at four things this encounter with Jesus reveals about the demons and their relationship to Jesus.
They know who He is
As soon as the see Him they recognize Him to be Jesus.
And they have more keen insight into eternal things than the townspeople and all of the people around.
Brothers and sisters, you can know who Jesus is, without knowing Jesus.
You might be in seminary.
But don’t assume because you can say who Jesus is, that you KNOW Him.
Knowing about Him is nothing more than memorizing facts.
And God is not impressed with your book smarts.
Even the demons believe—and shudder!” If your knowledge of God hasn’t led to a changed life, you are no better than demons.
He says, what have you to do with me?
Leave us alone!
They know Jesus has come to do away with all evil and to make all things new.
They know that in the end they will be destroyed and Jesus Christ will reign.
They know He is more powerful than them
This is meant to show that there are many of them.
Also, we see how strong they are.
Yet, they plead for mercy.
Have you ever seen dudes who talk a big game and walk around in circles but you never see them fight?
This is what we see here.
They realize that they have to ask for his permission.
Satan needs God’s permission to touch Job and is only allowed to do as much as God will permit.
Can you imagine what our world would be like if God didn’t restrain them?
But God doesn’t allow them to and we should praise Him for that.
Just like the scene before with “Peace, be still.” We see the authority of the Word of God.
He doesn’t ask, He commands.
The Word of Christ is powerful!
In the story before this He showed His Lordship over creation.
Jesus is Lord of all.
Brothers and sisters, we have to believe with firm conviction that Jesus is truly the Lord of Lords.
There is no tree, no sound, no insect, no animal, no atheist, no Muslim, no Hindu, no angel, no demon, no devil that is not inferior and under the authority of Jesus the Christ.
By his powerful word Jesus has delivered the man.
When the people see him he was sitting there clothed and in his right mind.
The last time they saw him he was bruising himself and continually screaming.
By His mercy Jesus restored this man.
He is reconciling all things to the Father.
This man, though He was oppressed, was a sinner like you and me.
But Jesus doesn’t deliver based on what you deserve.
Jesus is merciful because Jesus is merciful.
There is no spiritual being so strong, no sin so strong, nothing that surpasses the power and authority of Jesus.
Jesus is your healer and your deliverer.
Not only has the demoniac had an encounter with Jesus, but the whole town has.
How does everybody respond to their encounter with Jesus?
They had never seen such power and authority.
They didn’t know how to respond to him!
Many people today don’t like Jesus.
Jesus tends to make people uncomfortable.
If you’re looking for Him, you’ll keep searching your entire life.
He wants to follow Jesus and begs to be with him.
He obeys Him.
When was the last time you told others what the Lord has done for you?
Doesn’t have to be dramatic like demonic possession, but it’s good.
Could be overcoming temptation.
Or just telling others how He’s saved you.
We see here that it is possible for us to witness the power, grace, and authority of Jesus and to still not be a disciple.
None of these are the response God calls for.
But He would rise with all power.
He embarrassed them.
He erased their influence.
Now all of their rebellion is in vain.
There was no way the other guys could win, but they kept pushing along deceiving themselves.
He began the work promised of old.
Only by responding to His work on the cross with repentance and faith do we get to benefit from the authority, power, and triumph of Jesus.
Examine your own hearts and see how you respond to this Lord.
