Well I think we can find the answer to this question in Romans 12.
After comprehensively laying out the doctrines of the Gospel in Romans 1-11, Paul turns to tell us how we should now live.
Now in chapter 12, Paul makes a total shift to tell us what we should do.
So I want us to look at this passage, and look at three things we should do in light of what God has revealed here.
So when Paul makes this shift, he appeals to us.
But before we look at what he is urges us to do, I want to look at the way he appeals to us.
To tell us how we should live Paul doesn’t say we should live a certain way because “We want to be the greatest or holiest,” or because “this is how upstanding members of society should live,” or, “our mothers taught us better than that,” or even, “so that God will like you.” No, he gives us a different kind of motivation.
Recently I talked to a woman who was part owner of business that she and her partner started several years ago.
Then she told me about all the kids she had and how much she loved them.
And she pointed to those as her primary loves, and her motivation for doing everything that she does.
She loves her kids and her job.
For the Christian our motivation never stops with anything in this world.
But none of those should be our final motivation.
Here Paul points to something outside of ourselves and our earthly lives for motivation.
He appeals to us by the mercies of God.
Paul might be thinking of the glorious Gospel God has given us.
