One thing that’s not unique to hip hop, but is more common is the remix.
You keep some elements from the original, but you also change some things to put a new spin on it.
But one of the goals is to give the listener something new and even improve on the song.
I say that because God promises to make a new covenant with His people, but everything about it isn’t new.
But there are some things about this covenant that are totally different.
And we read about this New Covenant in Jeremiah 31.
And He delivers them in dramatic fashion with ten plagues and turning Pharaoh’s heart, and splitting the Red Sea.
He says, “Now therefore, if you will indeed obey my voice and keep my covenant, you shall be my treasured possession among all peoples…” (Exodus 19:5).
He gives them 10 commandments that they are to follow.
Just do what He says and everything will be fine.
The problem is they didn’t obey Him.
And so we find ourselves in the time of Jeremiah.
One day after all of this destruction, He is going to restore them.
And that’s what we’re going to think about today.
Listen to what He says.
“I will put my law in their minds and write it on their hearts.
I just want to point to four things we should be grateful for and praise God for as we reflect on this text.
So how is it that the New Covenant is God working internationally?
And In the context of the book of Jeremiah, this new covenant is also being made the nation of Israel.
And when Jesus comes to Earth, His focus is clearly on the Jews.
He says to “make disciples,” not just of Israel, but of all nations.
He even sends the most unlikely Apostle, Paul, to specifically to the Gentiles.
And we should praise God for this!
And He continues to be gracious by offering salvation to all of us.
And at first it looked like water was only available to the children.
It was great that the children could be saved, but now we find out all of us can.
But God graciously offers all of us the salvation that we need.
Some of us are here tonight.
So for some of us this should be especially praiseworthy.
As Ephesians 2 says, “Therefore remember that at one time you Gentiles in the flesh… remember that you were at that time separated from Christ, alienated from the commonwealth of Israel and strangers to the covenants of promise, having no hope and without God in the world.
Praise God that He didn’t leave us far off, utterly hopeless, and without Him.
And we know where the story ends.
Because “by blood ransomed people for God from every tribe and language and people and nation” (Rev 5:9).
As God’s people, we should be trying to do life together in a way that shows off God’s plan to save a diverse people.
And I love to look up there and see diverse people giving testimonies.
That could be said of us here today.
You’re not saved by your age or race.
Scripture is clear that “everyone who calls upon the name of the Lord shall be saved.” Not just rich people, or poor people, or old people or young people, or Jewish people or white people or black people.
That’s one of the glories of the Gospel that God would save any sinner who puts their faith in Christ.
As a church, we may not all come from the same place and we may not have the same ethnic background, but we have something greater in common.
This is why some of us need to pack up our bags and go!
Someone told us and someone needs to go tell them.
Let’s praise God for working internationally.
In what other ways will this covenant be new?
God Is Working Internally
I don’t think the text is trying to draw some huge difference here between heart and mind.
Other translations say “I will put my law within them and I will write it on their hearts.”
We’re accustomed to thinking of the heart as a warm, fuzzy place inside.
But, biblically the heart is a lot more than just a mushy emotional place.
It includes our emotions, but it also includes our minds, our will, and our desires – everything that happens inside.
We use the word heart.
It’s like the GPS system that directs our actions.
And this is why God’s law, as perfect as it was, could never save us.
It seems like today, a lot of the arguments in favor of living immoral lives has to do with what’s natural.
The truth is our hearts are so broken and confused, that wrong feels right and right feels wrong.
When I was growing up watching foul music videos, it felt right to lust after those women on the screen.
Our hearts trick us.
It wasn’t enough to have the law, because our sinful hearts only respond in rebellion.
This internal work is at the core of this new covenant and we should praise God for it.
Philippians 2 tells us, “Work out your own salvation with fear and trembling…” Why?
It’s because of his work inside of us that we will work out our salvation.
So in light of this truth, that’s exactly what we should be doing.
We should be confident in our fight against sin, knowing that God is at work within us.
He’s actually working in our hearts to help us trust and obey.
Maybe today you’re struggling with some sin that you feel like you can’t defeat.
In my own life, I can see this so clearly.
I can remember being in love with my sin.
Even before I started changing my behavior, I noticed that the way I felt, and how I thought, and what I desired was changing.
And there’s nothing sweeter than watching God do that in others.
This is a unique kind of unity that only Christians care share.
We should praise God for His internal work.
This covenant is rooted in a personal relationship with His people.
He is our God.
When you do that, you’re just promising to pay your landlord rent each month.
There’s a personal, committed love and affection there in all of His actions toward His people.
He’s purchased us with the blood of His Son and we belong to Him.
Bought with the precious blood of Christ.” God has always sought to have a personal relationship with His people, and this was to continue with this new covenant.
When you think of God is He merely the Creator up there?
Is He the Lover of your soul?
The people of Israel were not living as if He was their God.
They weren’t trusting His Word.
And nothing about their life said they were His people.
God has stamped His name on us.
When Jesus went to the cross as our high priest, He gave us access to the God of the Universe.
I think about Exodus where His people were enslaved and the text says, “He heard their cries and remembered His covenant with their fathers.” Christian, God has committed Himself to us in a way that He hasn’t committed Himself to all people.
He is your God.
I wonder how much more comfort we would feel in the midst of trial if we grasped the truth that He is our God and we are His people.
I think about Joseph being sold into slavery, and saying that what his brothers meant for evil, God meant for good.
I think in my own life, the times when I don’t grasp this, I do exactly what Brad called us not to do this morning.
God is on my side.
He cares about my holiness more than I do.
I am one of His people.
But it is crucial, that we grab a hold of this.
And we should praise Him for this interpersonal work of salvation.
God Is At Work
Brothers and sisters, this New Covenant does not ultimately depend on you.
God is the one who had the plan. God is the one who was merciful and didn’t take us out the first time we sinned.
God was the one who poured His wrath out on Christ.
This covenant begins and ends with God.
And He has committed Himself to that people.
Because we know that even our disobedience can’t break this covenant.
These are beautiful blessings that are part of this New Covenant.
And we enter into covenant with God when we trust in the life, death, and resurrection of His Son, Jesus Christ.
Instead of pouring out His wrath, God will pour out unsearchable riches on your head for an eternity.
And the truth is none of these aspects of the New Covenant we talked about will be realized perfectly until we are with Him.
When we are with Him we will be a people with perfect, pure hearts that love Christ and all of His ways.
Until then we press on and strive to trust Him more and more each day.
I was impressed by their athletic excellence, but I was even more impressed when I thought about the disciplined training it must have taken to get there.
But according to the Bible, there’s a superior kind of training—not because physical training is worthless, but because it’s limited.
So while you’re running, doing drills, and lifting weights, rest in the knowledge that it’s a good thing to do.
This is what the Apostle Paul said to Timothy:
We could sum up Paul’s thoughts on working out like this: Physical training is good, but spiritual training is better.
When Paul talks about “training for godliness” I think he means doing the things that make you spiritually stronger.
Maybe lifting our Bible is even less desirable than lifting a weight, but neglecting it means we miss out on more than good health.
We all know that when something lasts longer it’s more desirable.
We’d rather buy something that lasts a while, because we’ll get more out of the investment.
How often do you think about your eternity?
There’s no question that all of us will live forever; the question is what that forever will be like.
This should impact what we focus on from day to day.
Your physical strength may get you into college, but it won’t get you into Heaven.
How healthy is your soul?
God enables us to build “spiritual muscle” when we let go of sin and grab a hold of Jesus.
Athletes train hard because they want to be prepared for the game or match ahead of them.
All of us will face spiritual tests, trials, and battles everyday.
Coasting doesn’t work for athletes and it won’t work for Christians.
We’ll only make it through this marathon by God’s grace, but he prepares us for the race through our spiritual exercise.
On those days when I don’t feel like praying or reading God’s Word, I can remind myself that the benefit isn’t small or temporary.
That’s all the motivation I need.
