His life was really interesting from beginning to end.
This really had a strong impact on him, because he always felt abandoned, and he later abandoned one of his own children.
And they really took him in and treated him like their own son.
And his parents made great sacrifices to give him every chance to succeed.
They shaped him.
The beautiful thing about adoption is that people who have no responsibility to somebody decide to take on responsibility.
The child is needy and they agree to meet those needs.
God is not obligated.
We are desperately needy, and God has promised to meet those needs.
God is adopting rebels who have offended him.
God is adopting beautiful but broken sinners.
Let’s look at John 1:10 to start our time together.
He came to his own, and his own people did not receive him.
One of the most common assumptions in a country like ours is that everyone is a child of God.
The Bible teaches that when we’re born, we’re not children of God, but enemies of God.
This doesn’t mean that we have horns, but that we’re following the devil and that our actions look more like his than like God’s.
“Of course we’re all children of God,” they might say.
It’s similar to the difference between being a child’s biological father, but not actually being in their life, compared to being someone’s dad, loving them, playing with them, and taking care of their needs.
There has to be a change, a new birth, a conversion.
If we’re going to become a son or daughter of God, we have to be adopted into his family.
If you consider yourself to be a Christian, I want you to ask yourself why.
And they usually say yes, and I’ll ask them why.
And how do you receive Jesus?
So imagine God as this wealthy philanthropist who offers to adopt every single human being on the Earth as his child.
And you’re in the family.
Now we notice he doesn’t just say “believing in his teaching.” He says “believing in his name.” There is a very big difference between believing in someone’s teaching and trusting in an actual individual.
So friends, I want you to know that it is not enough for you to say, “I believe in some of Jesus’ teaching, I believe we should love one another, I believe we should go to church.” John is saying receiving him means believing in his name!
All of Jesus.
Sometimes we think that faith is agreeing with some facts about Jesus.
But faith is actually trusting him.
Have you bet your life on this?
You notice he tells us a little bit about what it means to be a child of God, describing them as those “who were born, not of blood nor of the will of the flesh nor of the will of man, but of God.”
That means we’re made new.
The first time we were born we weren’t right, so we need to be born all over again.
He gives several qualifiers to make sure we understand that.
“Not of flesh”—he wants us to know it did not originate with human sexual desire.
This new birth is of God!
It’s a reality for those who’ve trusted in Christ.
The assumption that you’ll never be able to get past that sin is a faulty assumption.
Something new has occurred.
You’ve joined a new team with new goals.
So you have family members, brothers and sisters in the faith.
Encourage them.
When a family adopts someone, they go to great lengths to make sure the adopted child doesn’t feel like they’re the adopted child.
Instead they want to treat them like their other children.
Have you ever heard someone talk about a trust fund baby?
So people resent them, because everyone else has had to work hard for the things they have.
On the other hand, this rich kid never had to work a day in his life but he’s doing better than everyone else.
We’ve lived contrary to his law, and we’ve disobeyed him, but when we put our faith in Christ we get all the benefits Jesus received.
Let’s talk about a few of those things.
Righteousness “For our sake he made him to be sin who knew no sin, so that in him we might become the righteousness of God.” (2 Corinthians 5:21)
He was treated like us, and we get treated like him.
When you’re adopted by God, you’re seen as righteous in him—not just as a dirty, worthless sinner, but as righteous.
We get the A+ even though we failed, and Jesus suffers the punishment for failing.
And then that keeps me from praying, because I feel like he doesn’t want to hear from me.
So he’s not mad at me anymore; he’s pleased with me in Christ.
I didn’t do what it takes to get there, and there’s nothing I can do that will remove me.
This also frees me from the bondage of trying to please other people all the time.
And the thing that helps me most is to remember that I don’t need their approval, because I already have God’s.
God thinks highly of you in Christ, so let that be enough.
We’ve earned eternal death, but in Christ we get to live forever with God.
We get to live in perfect worship of the King forever.
Why do we live like that?
This is the time we have on this earth to glorify God until we’re with him in heaven.
But when we know this is just the preview we can spend our time investing in our eternity.
Fatherly Love & Care
And I would do anything for him.
Further proof of the fact that we are treated as true sons of God is the fact that we can never be separated from His love.
Because we are in Jesus we can never be separated from God.
Answer: No one can separate God’s children from His love.
There is nothing in all of Creation that can separate us from God’s love in Christ.
When you were a kid, did you ever have someone give you something, then try to take it back?
She would have something that she didn’t really like, and then she would give it to me.
It’s similar with God.
He says, “Mine!” No one can snatch us away from him and his love.
None of our hard times can separate us from God’s love.
