All We Need is in God — TRIP LEE - OFFICIAL SITE
We don’t have to turn there.
Now I know we hear Christians say all the time that God is enough, that God is all we need, but it doesn’t always feel real.
We live in a fallen world, with big issues.
What does it look like, practically, even when everything is crashing down around us?
This kind of trouble and distress isn’t foreign to God or His Word.
David wrote this Psalm as well.
Before we read the text though, I want to give some background.
David is one of the most well known men in the history of the world.
He basically just threw some rocks at him and slayed him.
He was becoming more popular than the king, and the king didn’t like it.
And he began to do everything he could to kill him.
He’s a war hero who’s been turned into a fugitive.
It’s David against the king and his entire army.
He fears for his life, and it’s in that distress that he cries out to God.
Then the righteous will gather about me because of your goodness to me.
We’re going to walk through this text.
And as we walk through it, we’ll see that even in times of horrible trouble, we can find everything we need in God.
The God of the universe cares about this shepherd fugitive.
Who needs an invisible friend, when everything in your life is chaos?
The fact that God is a friend, that He cares, makes all the difference in the world.
For example, what would happen if my cable went out, and instead of calling my cable company I called my Internet provider.
I can remind them how I’ve been a faithful customer, and impress on them how appalled I am at this atrocious service.
And they’re not going to try to do anything about it.
It matters whether or not someone cares.
We could cry out to God all day long, but it wouldn’t make any difference at all.
His life is in jeopardy.
It’s like he’s being chased by thugs with guns.
I can imagine David in the cave, sweating as he prays this prayer.
And that truth drives the way he prays.
Look at verse six.
David says this over and over in the Psalms.
So hear me.
Like, “Lord, listen to your beloved.
David knew about God’s track record.
And he expected that this God would hear him this time as well.
Or do you imagine you’re just saying words?
Is prayer a real interaction with God or are you just throwing words up at the ceiling?
David is actually airing his complaints to God.
But here David shows us the right way to voice our complaints.
He doesn’t complain in a way that puts God’s character or judgment into question.
It’s OK to air your complaints to God, but do it with full confidence in Him.
But when we accuse him of being unjust – whether out loud or in our hearts – that’s when it becomes sin.
One rapper, in a song called Dear God, talked about prayer like this.
If God knows everything, what’s the point of telling him?
It bothers me when Christians talk about prayer as if the main goal is aligning your will with God’s.
We tell him because He hears us, He delights in hearing from us, and He cares and He always responds in love (more on that later).
Lift up your voice.
When my spirit grows faint within me, it is you who know my way.
Twice he tried to kill him when he played the harp for him.
Saul gave him his daughter purposely, as a snare to him.
And now Saul and his armies are pursuing him.
I know some of us feel like we can relate to David here.
We feel like our entire lives are a mess.
This is how David feels.
It’s God who knows what He’s going through.
Look to my right and see; no one is concerned for me.
d. No One But God
He’s unwelcome in the kingdom.
Yet he goes before God. David knows God is his friend and that God will care for him.
And your neighbors don’t even know who you are.
He knows the pain and turmoil you’re experiencing.
Often we spend our lives trying to be noticed by other people, wanting their attention and respect.
God is with you.
It would be depressing if this Psalm ended here.
But David knows that even when he feels the most alone and abandoned, God cares.
So we can find everything we need in God, even when it’s rough.
I want to bring your attention to one more thing he says in verse four.
We think he means he has no one to run to.
He means I have no home.
I have nowhere to sleep.
He didn’t have the option of checking in to a Motel 6.
So we can find everything we need in God?
Look at verse five.
First he was saying, I don’t have a friend, but you’re my friend.
I don’t have a caregiver, but you’ll take care of me.
You’re my refuge.
I wanted it to be more to us than a cute coffee cup verse.
We saw this refuge language last week in Psalm 141 too.
God is his refuge.
I think it means He is your protection from all the things that threaten you in the outside world.
It’s to protect us from harm – from weather, from thieves, from attackers.
David is saying he will hide in God.
More specifically, He’s saying in God his soul takes refuge.
What is there in your life, that if you lost it you would feel exposed and open to destruction?
Is it your status?
If so, then it doesn’t sound like God is your refuge.
It’s a deep trust in God as our protection.
David knows his life is in danger, and he’s distressed, yet he feels safe.
Now am I saying, that because God is our protection, He won’t allow us to be hit with any trials?
Think about it like this.
Now that doesn’t mean that if I walk outside in the rain or leave it on while I’m washing dishes, it won’t get wet.
This is similar for the believer.
We can get hit by the storm, but we cannot be destroyed.
Romans 8 lays out the most awful things we can imagine happening to us, including death.
Romans 8 also tells us that everything will turn out for the good of those who love God and are called according to His purpose.
And that should comfort us.
He is our protection.
So again I ask where do you run in troubled times?
We’re running to false shelters.
No matter what that false shelter is, whether it’s a person or an addiction – it’s dangerous.
They seem safe, but in the end they only leave us more exposed.
Also, don’t make the mistake of thinking you can just make God your refuge when it’s time.
You live in the house, and you’re protected when it starts to rain.
Now as we talk about God’s protection, we can’t overlook the fact that a couple days ago a horrible thing happened in Newtown, Connecticut.
It was very young kids five and six year olds.
And tragedies like this make people ask questions.
Why didn’t He protect those innocent kids?
What would I say?
Why not me instead?
I don’t want to give some philosophical answer about the problem of evil.
I think I would just say I don’t know why, but I do know God.
God hasn’t told us all the specifics about why He allows certain things, but He has told us what He’s like.
He’s already proven that.
Because even in the most heart wrenching of situations, we can find everything we need in God.
It could mean a particular reward that’s rightfully yours.
He’s away from his home and no longer in the king’s good graces.
My wife and I were watching this documentary on ESPN this week called Broke.
Some of these guys literally made hundreds of millions, but blew it all on houses, cars, clothes, and an expensive lifestyle.
And when they lost all of it, they didn’t know what to do.
Then there’s a guy like Paul the Apostle.
Do you want to know the secret?
All he wanted was Christ.
God was His treasure.
Our joy and peace can’t go up and down with how financially stable we feel, which is a temptation sometimes.
You can trust in money, but it’s a bad idea.
Money won’t last, but God will.
How can you do your work in a way that prioritizes God over money?
If so, God may not truly be your treasure.
Now saying that God is all that we really need is not to say all of our other needs disappear.
But it is a call to depend more on your greatest need.
I know there are some of us here today who don’t have everything we want, and maybe not everything we need.
And praise God that your greatest need has already been met in Christ.
Every day you look up there’s a new superhero movie.
Then you have other kinds of action movies like Taken and James Bond.
There’s nothing that he or anyone else can do to save him.
And it’s in this trouble that he goes to God.
My son cries out for food, because he feels the hunger and knows he needs food.
He knows that those who pursue him are too strong for him.
And you notice how David asks.
In verse one he says he’s asking for “mercy.” He’s asking that he would not get what he deserves, but instead something he doesn’t deserve: deliverance.
Humbly recognizing that you’re too weak.
But we’re asking God to mercifully deliver us.
Obviously, I don’t want to just say “God is enough” and belittle their situation.
As a church this a way we can serve each other in times of desperate need.
Pray for them, love them.
The same is true of us, yet we run elsewhere sometimes.
God reveals Himself over and over again in Scripture as a deliverer.
What about in the life of Joseph?
When all looks impossible, God can still deliver.
Armies fall over, mountains bow down, and winds and waves halt in His presence.
He even went to great lengths to deliver us from sin.
The prayer in this psalm, with its distress and urgency, reminds me of another prayer in the Bible.
It reminds me of Jesus’ prayer in the Garden of Gethsemane.
And He pleads with God, and basically asks him if there’s another way.
And Jesus did not try escape.
Jesus died on the cross.
He was taking punishment for sins that He didn’t commit.
He wasn’t a sinner, yet He died a sinner’s death on that cross.
And He’s called us, not to deliver ourselves, but to trust in the Hero who came to get us.
Give your life to Him.
He has not promised protection.
Christians do go through troubled times, but our story ends with deliverance.
I can think of many, many times when I felt in distress and pleaded with God for mercy.
And God answered my prayer.
And I totally forgot about God.
I didn’t honor Him.
And even forgot about what He did, which seems absurd.
And my birthday was around this time too so it was extra special.
And I think I probably shed tears of joy.
How strange is it, that we can plead with God to have mercy on us, but days later forget as if it never happened?
He means as he praises God for His goodness, other believers will join Him.
We hear of God’s goodness and we gather around one another and praise Him together.
It really didn’t matter what happened, Mom and Dad could fix it.
I just went to Mom and Dad, because they got me.
We need this kind of childlike faith.
