I love the game of basketball, but I’m a lot better at watching it than playing it.
The stands were always full for home games.
Sadly, they probably made as much of a difference in the game as I did.
I think he realized that both of us were just there to watch, but I happened to have a better seat.
I like to tell myself they saved me for those final seconds so I could secure the win.
When I emerged from the locker room after my shower, nobody wanted a picture with me.
Nobody on either team envied me.
I was the guy who never made it into the game, and nobody envies the benchwarmer.
I can’t ever remember somebody shooting a pretend air jumper and calling out the name of an unknown role player.
You’ll never forget when Jordan switched hands in midair or when Ray Allen hit that three at the buzzer.
They’re in the action at those key moments, not just spectating.
If you showed up on a typical college campus and interviewed students about their goals for the semester, you wouldn’t hear much about living for the King.
All of us have goals and desires, but God isn’t always in them.
I’m not a movie critic trashing the acting; I’m a supporting actor trying to get my lines right too.
I wanted to be there so badly, but I couldn’t make it because it fell during the first week of my new job.
Especially when I saw them posting pictures online of their amazing experiences.
It’s easy to envy others, especially when they’re doing something great like my friends were doing.
And that makes following Jesus feel like getting taken out of the game, not getting subbed in.
WHY DO WE THINK WE’RE MISSING OUT?
One of our problems is that we can only see what’s right in front of us.
It’s like a camera when the things in front are in focus and everything behind is blurry.
There’s not a person on this earth who doesn’t struggle to see clearly.
But when you throw youthfulness into the equation, it adds up to disaster.
It’s not surprising.
Our lives have been brief and we’ve only seen so much.
This doesn’t mean we should flog ourselves in shame; but it does mean we should be aware, because sinful shortsightedness can lead to disaster.
The Christmas after my first child was born, a terrible snowstorm hit as my wife and I drove back to DC from Pittsburgh.
My knuckles turned white as I nervously gripped the wheel; I realized the lives of my wife and son were in my hands.
I could only see a few inches in front of my face, so I literally didn’t know where I was going.
That’s not a good feeling.
You can understand why it was my least favorite drive ever.
Have you ever thought about how disastrous shortsightedness can be in our lives?
Too many of us are trying to live our lives with no regard for what happens later.
Sleeping with your boyfriend or girlfriend may seem like a win in the moment, but that kind of intimacy was never meant to be enjoyed apart from the unique union of a marriage.
Every decision we make is a small piece of a larger puzzle.
It’s tragic to treasure a moment in time more than an entire lifetime.
