Even When — TRIP LEE - OFFICIAL SITE
This kind of unchallenged reign is what usually comes to mind first when we think “winner.” But I want us to leave room for another kind of winner.
The kind of winner whose weaknesses are more noteworthy than his strengths.
The last two years have been amazing in many ways, but they’ve also been pretty hard.
I’ve experience the joy of pastoral minsitry in a healthy church.
It’s hard for outsiders to tell sometimes because I seem to be doing so many good things.
Maybe that’s why when it hit me it felt more like a sucker punch than an organized bout.
Over the next year it did get better, but it still left me a shell of what I used to be.
I honestly can’t remember the last time I felt rested— like my battery died, but never recharged.
We need it for every task, every conversation, every thought.
For some reason, my body won’t let me refuel.
It’s left me limping through every area of my life.
Some may look at the missed deadlines, the discouraging days, the occasional tension in my marriage, and think, “Man, this guy is losing right now.” It sure feels like losing when I have to change my life plans yet again or tell my son, “Daddy’s too tired to play right now.” I would be lying if I said it wasn’t discouraging and trying.
No, I’m not the superhuman strong kind of winner; I’m actually pretty weak.
And I suspect many of you are too.
I do have victory, but not because my strength has secured it.
I’m not the victor who defeated foes and secured the win; I really just received it.
Paul puts it like this in Romans:
Paul quotes Psalm 44, where the Psalmist cries out for God’s help in the midst of trial.
One of the most striking things is that after mentioning terrible things: being killed, being slaughtered, Paul says even in those things we are conquerors.
Even when we’re being conquered by this fallen world, we remain conquerors through Jesus.
Doesn’t that bring you incredible hope?
Maybe, like me, you’re in a season of great difficulty.
When I spend my day focusing on my weakness and grumbling in my heart, I only feed my discouragement and fuel my false sense of hopelessness.
When he was defeating his foes, he was defeating them on our behalf.
Don’t Just Look at Now
But Jesus purchased unbelievable things for me on the cross that I haven’t expereienced yet.
He’ll even give me a brand new body and wipe away all my tears.
Those who don’t have the same trials and setbacks that always seem to plague us.
Many people seem to be winning the daily battles, but they haven’t trusted in the One who’s won the war.
But there are bigger wins to be had.
He’s defeated all of our enemies for us: sin, Satan, and death itself.
Anyone, even the weakest among us can be winners.
The victor ain’t the one that’s winning seventh inning
