Only God Can Judge Me?
Conversations can get real tense real quick though—especially when we’re talking about controversial issues.
So I try to be careful with my words, and simply point to what God says, rather than my own opinions.
You conveniently skipped over that verse, huh?” It never fails.
Misunderstanding this verse means we misunderstand how we’re supposed to love one another.
What He Doesn’t Mean
As we walked through every nook and cranny of our marriage, we had to discuss the good, the bad, and the ugly.
During our discussions, my wife helped me to see areas where I’m falling short, where I’m not being as loving as I should be.
I don’t think so.
Just a few verses later, Jesus tells his listeners to make judgments about who they share the truth with.
Not to mention the numerous other passages in Scripture that command us to make varying types of moral judgments.
And if we’re honest with ourselves, we don’t mean that either.
They don’t think it’s wrong to call the cops on the drug dealers selling dope in front of their house.
In each of these cases, judgments are being made about the actions of others.
Who are we to make judgments about others?
We could be talking about judging a talent show or judging a court case.
Similarly, the Greek word Jesus uses for “judge” doesn’t always mean the same thing.
As you continue reading the passage, it becomes clear that Jesus is attacking a specific type of judgment.
That’s like a naked man criticizing the hole in your jeans.
In verse 5 he says, “You hypocrite, first take the log our of your own eye.” Before we attempt to judge others, we must judge ourselves.
And while self-righteous people have a special gift for pointing out your flaws, they’re oblivious to their own.
And he tells us God will judge us in return.
You notice he never says, “Take the log out, and don’t worry about other people’s eyes.” He actually says, “First take the log out of your own eye, and then you will see clearly to take the speck out of your brothers eye.” He’s telling us to examine ourselves, and then to help the brother out.
Why would I let someone I love continue to live in a destructive manner?
But it must be based on God’s standard, not my own.
Let’s check out hearts, strive to learn this concept, daily and apply it.
