He ate nothing during those days, and at the end of them he was hungry.
First, lets discuss exactly where we find ourselves in Lukeʼs gospel.
John has been baptizing a lot of people, but when Jesus is baptized something different happens.
After telling us about this declaration from God, and before telling us what the baptized Son of God will do, Like gives us a genealogy, telling us about Jesusʼ ancestry.
So here we have the Lord Jesus, who is not only the divine Son of God, but He is also a descendant of Adam, the very first man.
Jesus is the God-man.
As we look at these two verses, we are going to look at three things we should remember when we face temptation.
God Allows and Uses Temptation As Part of His Plan (1-2a)
So following Jesus’ baptism, where he was declared the Son of God with whom the Father was well pleased, you might expect that the Spirit of God would lead Jesus to dethrone the evil King Herod, or maybe send him directly to begin his ministry of preaching and doing signs and miracles.
Instead the Holy Spirit leads Him to the desert.
Now surely the sovereign, all-knowing Holy Spirit knows that He is leading Jesus to the very place where Satan will tempt Him – in the same way that the Holy Spirit knew in Acts 20, that He was leading Paul to Jerusalem, and that Paul would be persecuted and imprisoned there.
This situation is no different.
Why would the Holy Spirit send Jesus to the desert where he will be tempted?
This conflict in the wilderness gives Jesus the stage to show Himself to be the God-man.
Lebron James is the MVP of the league, but his team canʼt win the championship.
And when they team up they throw huge celebrations, boasting that, because of all the talent they have, they will win 5, 6, 7 – maybe even more – championships as a team.
Now when the season starts they have to show up and prove themselves.
And the media watched and scrutinized their every move to see if they were the historic team everyone hoped.
Will he please God?
He was made in the image of God, to reflect Godʼs glory in a unique way.
We see Israel in the wilderness failing to obey God.
Will Jesus be able to do what Israel could not do?
And Jesus does prove to be who He was proclaimed to be.
And the Holy Spirit sent Him there to do just that.
That is untrue.
Itʼs not something that slipped under the radar.
This temptation happens by permission of God.
Brothers and sisters, James makes very clear that when we are being tempted we are not being tempted by God.
God is grieved by the fact that there are evil forces in the world that try to contend with His power and derail you from the path of righteousness.
And He will use even them for His glory.
We think that God is only glorified in public obedience such as sharing the Gospel with our neighbors, or feeding the homeless, or preaching.
Both public and private obedience matter very much.
When we are in private, and we are being tempted to sin, God is glorified by our obedience.
It magnifies His goodness, and his trustworthiness, and His power to save sinners.
As the Lord Jesus is tempted, He is in a weak and vulnerable state.
At the end of those 40 days Jesus was hungry – which seems kind of obvious; think about denying yourself food for 40 whole days.
He was the God-man.
Jesus was a real man.
In this case, Satan tempts Jesus in a vulnerable moment as He is hungry.
He tempts Jesus with a more immediate reign, with food, with things that he could rightfully possess.
Jesus shows Himself here, to be the son of man who came not to be served, but to serve.
I read a quote this week that said, “Opportunity only comes knocking once, but temptation lays on the doorbell.” Temptation does not leave easily.
Like the villains in horror movies who chase the victims until they eventually trip or get too tired to run any more.
Well in a similar way, the world, the flesh, and the devil will not lay off of their lies in times of weakness.
So how was Jesus able to resist the temptation of the devil in His time of weakness?
Well, if we looked at the passage below, we would see that Jesus did what Adam did not do.
And Jesus submitted His will to the will of the Father.
Because the truth is none of us are strong enough.
Resisting the temptation you will face next week begins this week.
What are you filling yourself with?
Jesus was weak.
We should seek, as Ephesians encourages, to be filled with – and possessed by – the Spirit of God.
And we should do this alongside our brothers and sisters in the faith.
We may be tired, or emotionally drained, or sick, or maybe weʼre so convinced of our strength that weʼre weak without knowing it.
During these vulnerable moments the offers of the tempter may seem more attractive, but God’s Word is still true, and Satan’s seduction is still a lie.
I am more like Jonah than Jesus.” Brothers and sisters, that is true of every Christian and non-Christian.
But the thing about the obedience of Jesus that we read about here is that Jesus was not just another man.
No, Jesus was the new Adam who came to represent us anew.
Jesus obeys where none of us could.
And this particular defeat, in the desert, was pointing to the victorious defeat of Satan that will happen at the end of Lukeʼs gospel.
On the cross Jesus paid for every single time that we have given in to temptation.
The devil is defeated.
Seeing the obedience of Jesus should not discourage us, it should encourage us.
Walk in the freedom purchased for you at the cross.
Jesus died and rose, that we would be able to resist the tempter, and look like Him.
When we are tempted, letʼs remember that God allows and uses temptation, that weakness is not an excuse to give in to temptation, and that the tempter has already been defeated.
For we do not have a high priest who is unable to sympathize with our weaknesses, but we have one who has been tempted in every way, just as we are —yet was without sin.
The Lord Jesus can sympathize with us.
So we can approach His throne with confidence, pleading for mercy and grace.
