Why I Married a White Girl — TRIP LEE - OFFICIAL SITE
I found women of all backgrounds beautiful, but black girls were my “preference.” But when I arrived on my college campus in 2006, I wasn’t looking for a wife at all.
My first album had just come out, so I had plenty of other things to focus on.
We ran in the same circles and we ended up joining the same church, so we saw each other a lot.
She really loved Jesus and she had this childlike willingness to do whatever He asked.
Over that first year, I watched her sacrifice countless hours of her time serving at our church.
Our conversation, whether serious or silly, always flowed with ease.
Don’t get me wrong, I thought she was beautiful from the first time I met her.
I just didn’t think I would.
My main preference was that my wife be godly, and Jessica was.
Never for a moment did I feel like I was settling.
I never wanted to value my preferences for a wife over what I needed in a wife.
I know some people who overlook a potential godly spouse because they don’t fit some random preference.
Some of our preferences may even be foolish, so we have to submit all of them to Scripture.
And as my wife and I begin to raise our first child, I couldn’t be more grateful for her.
It’s OK to want things in a spouse, but we have to submit our desires to what God wants for us in a spouse.
My husband is White and I am Black and I constantly get asked what made me choose someone outside of my race.
God is good!
