Last week after the events in Ferguson (and the responses to it on social media), I felt burdened to write a song.
So I started writing, and this is what came out.
I had a whole post ready on how Christians should respond in times like these, but after the news about Eric Garner yesterday, I decided to wait and post that later.
I watched most of the political conventions these last two weeks.
We also can’t afford to trust in a mere man to save the day.
They can not rid our world of all suffering, or bring perfect peace.
This is not an encouragement for you to ignore politics.
I encourage you to do the same.
The good life is not material prosperity, and political candidates aren’t stable enough to build your life upon.
If we paid attention in grade school Congress really runs the government and makes decision for the government so like you don’t disregard politics but pay attention and know in your heart that God is in control….
The power is (or at least, SHOULD BE) in the hands of the people, but we are giving away those freedoms to government and having them make decisions on our behalf…
People can and will say things to drum up emotion.
They have to hold true to those proclamations… just like we have to hold true to the Bible, Constitution, Declaration of Independence, Bill of Rights, etc.
I disagree that the choice between the two, if our focus is that narrow, is easy.
If the big issues don’t line up, I doubt that the less obvious ones will… in terms of government or government programs, what do you feel is their role?
The first thing to note before a discussion like this is to differentiate between the Church and State.
That being said, biblically speaking, it looks to me as if many programs that the government currently runs actually do not fall under its jurisdiction as well intentioned as they might be.
Here are a few examples.
Education falls to the family (Duet 6) and then to the Church to which God has appointed teachers and pastors (or Levites in the OT).
But Jesus didn’t say he would ask what your views were on homosexuality, marriage, or abortion when he returns.
So therefore which candidate do you think is most likely to follow those mandates…
He has spoken VERY clearly on those issues you minimize!
An YES, YOU and I should feed the hungry, cloth the naked, and care for widows and orphans, etc….ESPECIALLY those who belong to the household of faith….but where does Jesus tell you to appoint the government to do it??
Marriage is about Christ and the church…should we prioritize a welfare state and sexual rebels’ self interest above that?
First, I am NOT saying that a Christian SHOULD vote for Romney.
Now, having said that, the only excusable reason for a Christian to have voted for Obama is that he/she was uninformed of the issues and the person.
Only a fool puts his faith in a person of this world to “save” them from poverty or any other ill in their life, but to ignore a party that reviles God, and insist on killing the innocent can not be ignored.
I put no faith in the Republicans but I place hope and prayer that the Lord will help them see the right path to follow.
I AM saying don’t vote.
Your action or inaction WILL affect the outcome.
The other seeks to prevent these and the destruction they bring, and to preserve what God has graciously given this nation.
If you vote according to the bible, than no candidate will fully meet your requirements.
On the left there are moral problems (gay marriage, abortion, etc.) News flash: the government is NOT the church.
If we oppose Gay marriage we should do so by giving the world great examples of heterosexual marriages, instead of divorce rates at above 50 percent.
Will making gay marriage illegal change hearts?
Will making abortions illegal help a woman raise a child?
Will they prevent either sin from occurring?
(Remember abortions occurred in ugly ways befor legalization occurred).
And is giving to orphanages really an ALTERNATIVE to stopping the killing of innocent babies??
And it is a simple FACT that laws DO curb behavior – that is why we have them!
How we live, what we speak, promote, and oppose, must ALL be according to TRUTH (including His moral law) – or we are NOT worshipping Him as we should, but are being conformed to the pattern of this evil world.
YES, Jesus DID die for His LAW to rule our hearts – that we would love Him with all our heart, mind, soul, and strength (the summation of the Law), and promote the knowledge of Him (according to TRUTH) as he has revealed Himself, and the love of Him (including His law). Why should we be indifferent to what laws we implement?
This is not according to His Spirit – but another.
If the platform has stances that are contrary to the Word, then that candidate is not getting my vote.
My trust is not in a man nor a political party; it’s in my Father in heaven who loves me.
Faith first ALWAYS!
We can’t look to a president to save us or our nation.
What do you think about the idea of supporting candidates according how to well they match up with Scripture’s take on issues such as just wars and the jurisdiction of the government vs that of the individual, the family, or the Church?
Well said… in regards to the candidates’ moral beliefs and support for certain moral issues facing this country, what role do you believe they play in selecting the candidate to vote for?
I don’t expect Trip to directly respond to your question, but I can take a crack at it if you want.
As a Christian, I want to vote for the best friend of the Church, who will see that our needs are addressed to boost society and restore some sense of light to the nation.
If this were a perfect society with that being the major exception, I would expect God to be merciful towards us and send messengers to persuade us to give up this sin.
Too often I see abortion and gay marriage as leverages that people use to force you to vote for or against a candidate, who, mind you, may not even have a great deal of influence over that policy (or may have bigger things on his plate).
Fact of the matter is we do not know what God will do to one person or one nation as a result of abortion.
Same goes for homosexuality and gay marriage.
(What about heterosexual adultery or fornication?
But what influence do I have on that other than to enforce some kind of surface change of behavior?
In essence, our American ethic should be that one can do whatever they want, as long as they don’t impede on someone else’s liberties.
Another issue with voting based on religious mandates is on interpretation: What if a politician was elected with a mandate to enact “Scriptural” laws, and he enacts them wrongly?
Any kind of imposition by the government, whether for religious or secular reasons, stops you from doing what you want.
But if the government goes too far, it may stop you from doing something you want or need to do in good conscience.
Since the emperor saw himself as Lord and demanded worship, proclaiming Jesus is Lord got you killed.
It opens up the door for God to work freely in your heart, because outward actions aren’t being required of you.
For instance, many in my community think they are doing God a favor by showing up in church, or that their frequent attendance means they are going to Heaven.
That’s my take on voting as an American Christian.
But politicians will not be able to do for society what we can as a strong Church.
(And if you think about it, you don’t want anyone in your way for anything you do in life, spiritual or secular.)
A candidate can change or support a particular stance just to gain votes and popularity; however that doesn’t dictate how the country is run.
However, the platform didn’t change and it already sided public opinion.
The same with conservatives.
God turns the head of “king” in the direction he wants it to go; however, we need to make sure that our vote does not support those policies that are contrary to the Word.
I think and this is my opinion that even if we vote for the better candidate God word will still stand as truth and the End will come just as he says it will.
WHAT MAKES ME WANT TO CRY is when I see dying widows, poor starving children, and disabled people out there……This message is not for them?
I on the other hand Love the GOSPEL and know this is for the weak, dying and poor and has NOTHING to do with money.
He says He will add the things we “need.”
That is when I asked God about people like this man and other prosperity preachers.
Mat 6:19-21 “Do not lay up for yourselves treasures on earth, where moth and rust destroy and where thieves break in and steal;”but lay up for yourselves treasures in heaven, where neither moth nor rust destroys and where thieves do not break in and steal.
I have to remember why I’m doing it.
And that’s just what goes on in our hearts.
Trying to convince somebody that they’re sinners in need of a Savior and that Jesus is the only way, seems like the worst crime in the universe to our “tolerant” culture.
So why should we preach the Gospel?
People hate us for it.
People say we’re clinging to an old outdated book with outdated ideas.
We gotta know why we’re doing it, or we’ll give up.
Is it because we’re bigots who think we’re better than everybody else?
And we see that glory in His Son.
We preach Christ so people can see glory.
Drug addicts get high because they don’t grasp the glory of God.
Young dudes in our communities shoot each other, because they don’t grasp the glory of God.
Depressed youth commit suicide because they have no hope, because they don’t grasp the glory of God.
Seeing the glory of God matters, and we want them to see it.
So let’s think about what it means to help people see God’s glory in a dark world, where people don’t even want to know Him.
The Apostle Paul knew what it was like to serve among people who hated him and his Gospel.
No, Paul lives in the real world with real difficulties – a world that was in many ways a lot more difficult than ours.
Paul’s second letter to the Corinthian church is in many ways a defense and description of his ministry in the face of opposition.
Therefore, having this ministry by the mercy of God, we do not lose heart.
We refuse to practice cunning or to tamper with God’s word, but by the open statement of the truth we would commend ourselves to everyone’s conscience in the sight of God.
4 In their case the god of this world has blinded the minds of the unbelievers, to keep them from seeing the light of the gospel of the glory of Christ, who is the image of God.
6 For God, who said, “Let light shine out of darkness,” has shone in our hearts to give the light of the knowledge of the glory of God in the face of Jesus Christ.
If you’re a believer in the Gospel, you’ve been called to be a minister of the Gospel.
We’ve all experienced it.
From seventh grade boys asking a girl to a dance, to not making in to that college you applied to, to not getting that job you so desperately wanted, rejection is hard.
So much so that in many of his letters he’s forced to defend his character and his methods and his message.
Can you imagine being in Paul’s position?
Doubt about you, and your motives, and your teaching.
But how does Paul respond?
“Therefore, having this ministry by the mercy of God, we do not lose heart.”
Paul says he doesn’t lose heart.
He hasn’t gotten depressed, and given up, and thrown in the towel or found something else to give his life to.
Which is amazing, considering what this rejection was like.
From being imprisoned, stoned, rejected.
Have you ever shared the Gospel with someone and had them reject it?
I can think of times when people said they don’t need God, or argued with me and told me God wasn’t real, or people who started to act weird and avoid me after I shared with them, even people who aggressively yelled at me and threatened me.
Maybe you’d be tempted to fight, but I can’t fight.
Who wants to keep running into a brick wall?
Why doesn’t he lose heart?
“Therefore, having this ministry by the mercy of God, we do not lose heart.”
Right before this in chapter 3, Paul spends a bunch of time explaining the glory of the New Covenant ministry.
Paul’s encouragement comes from the ministry itself, not how successful he feels at any given time.
And Paul knows that it is a mercy of God that he of all people would have this ministry.
As the people of God, our joy is not based on how people respond to the message.
It’s based on the glory of the calling itself.
I don’t mean that we shouldn’t be grieved at the darkness of our world.
They may reject the message, but that doesn’t make it any less glorious.
We’re not hiding things and being slimy in the way we operate.” He wants them to know he intends to live in a manner worthy of the Gospel.
On the contrary, in Christ we speak before God with sincerity, like men sent from God.”
Look, if you’re sharing the Gospel with people, but those same people see your life is a mess, you’re working against the Gospel.
Paul says, he refuses to be crafty or mess with the Word.
Paul will NOT do anything that tampers with or changes or waters down the Word of God.
I wish more preachers today would have this resolve, not to distort God’s Word.
When you’re thinking about what church you should be a part of, make sure they’re preaching the Word.
Too many people have built a name for themselves by distorting God’s Word.
I know sometimes all of us are tempted to do it though.
What are some of the ways you may be tempted to tamper with the Gospel?
Not talking about sin?
It’s kind of like this.
Don’t you hate that?
Nobody likes that.
In similar manner, people see the Gospel as unpalatable, not sweet enough to people’s taste buds.
They figure as long as they don’t totally get rid of it it’s fine.
This is a horrible thing to do!
You don’t feel the need to tamper with something unless you think it’s flawed or insufficient.
D. State The Truth Plainly and Let It Stand Alone
The Gospel is enough.
It doesn’t do anybody any good to trick them into professing faith.
If a woman has a beautiful face, why cover it with a veil?
Don’t try to put make up on the Gospel so it will be cute.
Even in the midst of rejection, we should not waiver.
So often it seems like they just don’t get it.
Beware the Work of Satan
There are many misconceptions about him.
Everything that happens is a result of his great power, and he can do whatever he wants whenever he wants to do it.
They falsely believe that Satan either doesn’t exist or that he’s powerless and inactive.
The truth is Satan does have power, enough power that he works against Paul and his ministry.
“And even if our gospel is veiled, it is veiled to those who are perishing.
It seems that one of the criticisms against Paul was that he was hiding his message from some, that he was keeping some people from seeing it.
This is clear in 1 Corinthians 1:18 when Paul says, “the message of the cross is foolishness to those who are perishing…” It looks stupid to them.
Those people don’t exist.
They will be punished with everlasting destruction and shut out from the presence of the Lord and from the majesty of his power on the day he comes to be glorified in his holy people and to be marveled at among all those who have believed.
This is not a game that we play.
Those who reject this message of the Gospel, reject God.
He’s called the god of this world or this age, and only this age.
Scripture says he prowls around the earth looking for someone he can devour.
So what does Paul say Satan does here?
This should be evidence of how amazing and important it is for us to see the glory of God.
Satan goes so far as to blind us to make sure we cannot see the glory of God in the Gospel.
The glory of God is his weight, his beauty, splendor, majesty, greatness, and brightness.
Some of us think Satan’s main goal is to trip us up with a pyramid or one eye symbolism in a rapper’s album artwork.
Satan’s main goal is to keep you from seeing the glory of God in Christ.
He wants us to be tied up with money, and popularity, and perfect relationships, and success.
But it’s not enough for him to distract us – he actually blinds us.
That’s why we as believers in Jesus, because we want people to see the glory of God, that they would trust Christ, and glorify God with their lives, preach the Gospel.
They’re blind.
It seems impossible.
If God can save you, He can save anyone!
They are blind, and they need to see.
This also shows us that when people reject the Gospel, it doesn’t mean the message is flawed any more than it means a flashlight is flawed if a blind man doesn’t notice you shining it in his eyes.
Don’t let rejection make you doubt the Word.
We don’t base our opinions of art on men who can’t see.
Paul says Satan blinds them from seeing “the light of the gospel of the glory of Christ, who is the image of God.” This is why preaching Christ shows people the glory of God, because Jesus Christ is the very image of God.
It means that Jesus, being God himself, is the perfect representation of the Father.
Jesus is God in physical form, a perfect image.
And that glory is seen in Jesus.
Satan is “the god of this world.” What are we supposed to do when he’s coming against us?
“For what we proclaim is not ourselves, but Jesus Christ as Lord, with ourselves as your servants for Jesus’ sake.”
And Christ is what we preach.
A. Preach Christ!
Proclaim Christ.
Tell people about Jesus.
Tell that person about Jesus.
Yes they have other needs.
If you truly want men to see the glory of God, it does them no good to preach other things.
If Jesus is not the foundation, the motivation, the fuel behind everything else we preach, what we’re doing won’t last.
We know from the rest of the text and elsewhere in the Scriptures that Paul is referring to the Gospel message.
If you’re here tonight and you do not know that Gospel message, then that should be your goal before you leave.
At the core of this Gospel message, is the truth that God is holy, main is sinful, Christ was perfect and died for sinners, and He rose from the grave.
That’s the message you’ve been called to preach.
You cannot die for anyone.
You can only preach Christ.
Don’t try to change hearts, tell them about the One who can.
Why the Gospel?
And it’s clear from this passage, that God’s glory is clearly seen in the Good News about His Son.
These two people had very different stories.
Another was a good girl, who hated God.
Someone else shared the Gospel with them.
We just need to do our job.
For the weapons of our warfare are not of the flesh but have divine power to destroy strongholds.
But when the Gospel isn’t present we aren’t proclaiming Christ, but self.
Whether you mean to or not, that’s preaching yourself.
Who have you been sharing with?
What do you say to your neighbors or your coworkers when you get a few minutes to talk about things?
That word translated as servants, literally means slave.
That’s amazing.
Though I am free and belong to no man, I make myself a slave to everyone, to win as many as possible.
Are you making yourself the servant of those who need to hear the Good News?
I hope not.
I feel like that sometimes.
We should build our lives around evangelism.
I wonder who in this room is going to another country to share the Good News?
There are people who are blind.
We preach Christ, so men can see God.
If the world being evangelized ultimately depends on us, it is literally impossible.
All we can do is preach and love on them.
Paul takes a second to remind us who God is.
This is not a God who’s trying to do something amazing for the first time.
This is not a God who was created.
The God we’re going to other people on behalf of is the same God who created absolutely everything from absolutely nothing.
Blind eyes are no match for our God.
That’s the point Paul is making.
Our hearts are like a big room, that is pitch black.
But the light still doesn’t come on.
Still no lights.
That once darkened room is now as bright as any room could ever be.
Christian, if you’ve seen the glory of God in the face of Jesus, if you’ve turned from darkness to light, it is not your doing.
It’s God’s doing.
While the god of this world blinds us from the glory of Jesus, the true and living God shines light into hearts and opens blind eyes.
If your heart is no longer darkened how does that show up in your life?
Do you grasp what God has done for you?
If we were more captured, and enthralled with our God and what we have in Christ, we would tell other people more.
Do we tell them about Jesus?
Christian, take advantage of what God has done.
It doesn’t stop when you trust Him.
God displayed patience with us.
Blind men whose sight has been restored don’t walk around with blindfolds.
Conclusion Ministry in this fallen dark world has never been, and will never be easy.
We can learn, first, not to waiver because of the rejection of men.
Third, to embrace the work of the minister.
What do I do if I’m not preaching the Gospel as much as or how I should be?
Look to Christ.
And motivated to tell others so they can experience the same.
Those of us who have not put our faith in Christ, will perish.
But if we turn and trust in Christ, we get to see a glimpse of His glory now in the Gospel of Christ.
And we will gaze at His glory in full, forever.
