Proud Speech and the Glory of God
One of the reasons I love kids is because they show us ourselves.
Let me tell you what I mean.
This is really clear when kids do things they’re not supposed to do.
They’ll look around to see if you’re looking.
Why do they do that, though?
Adults are the same way.
You’re glad to speed until you see cops ahead.
Maybe some of us cheat on tests as long as the professor doesn’t catch us.
In 1 Samuel 2, Hannah prays in response to the birth of her son Samuel.
That’s what I think the main point of this verse is: If you talk proudly, you’ll have to answer to God for it.
But if you think your prideful words don’t matter, I’m afraid for you.
We won’t finally answer to our parents, or our friends, or our pastors, but to God.
And I think one of the ways that Hannah has in mind is what we see in chapter 1.
She wasn’t his only wife though.
Peninnah had children, but Hannah was barren.
And because of her barrenness and this taunting Hannah was grieved and she would weep.
Peninnah wasn’t just pointing out the fact that she had children.
She was taunting her, boasting over her, tormenting her.
How is it talking proudly?
It seems like she was assuming that somehow her bearing of children made her better than Hannah, which wasn’t true.
Peninnah was talking proudly, and she would have to answer to God for it.
Whether we recognize it or not, we all talk proudly and speak with arrogance in a variety of ways:
One obvious way is bragging about our accomplishments.
But we’re not praising God for it; the goal is making ourselves look better.
Maybe running into an old friend and they ask me what I’ve been doing.
Sometimes we slander others.
Maybe at work we point out that a particular co-worker is pretty lazy, so that people will notice the difference between you two.
We stand as a judge over them, because it makes us feel better about our spiritual accomplishments.
Maybe it’s a testimony on a Sunday night.
Or letting someone know you’ve been praying for them.
Even good things can be proud speech if your goal is exalting yourself.
So how do you know if it is?
Talking proudly is when you don’t acknowledge God as the One who’s sovereign and deserving of glory, in your heart or the words themselves.
And if we use our words to exalt ourselves, we’ll have to answer to Him for it.
Question 2: Why Do We Have to Answer to God for Proud Speech?
If only one of those last two phrases was true, it would change a lot.
God is at all times tuned in to every conversation that’s happening.
I cannot give myself any of your money without taking it from you.
Pride is an attack on God’s glory.
Peninnah was mistaken.
And we’re often guilty of the same.
Yes, God will reward our hard work, but that doesn’t guarantee anything.
What matters finally is not our strength, our wisdom, or even our faithfulness.
When you think in your heart that you deserve glory, that itself is a sin.
How could we, as sinful human beings, have the audacity, not only to think we’re worthy of God’s glory, but to actually present it that way to others?
Talking proudly is like not only robbing someone, but telling everyone about it and encouraging them to do the same.
God will not tolerate human beings trying to rob Him of His glory and encouraging others to do the same.
Prideful speech is picking a fight with God, which is why it’s inconsistent with the life of a believer.
If we’re not in Christ, our proud words, will be yet another reason for God to punish us.
If you’re not in Christ, there is nothing more terrifying than an all-knowing Judge.
He rose from the grave with all power, and calls men to follow Him.
But as Christians, what does it mean for our deeds to be weighed?
Christ has already taken our sins on the cross.
When we stand before God, our deeds will either testify to the fact that we’ve trusted in Christ or that we were too proud to trust in Christ alone.
Of course He’s pleased with us in Christ, but we want Him to be pleased with our good works that come out of faith in Christ.
We should not allow arrogant words to come from our mouths.
It’s like telling someone with a cold the solution is just to hold their sneeze in.
The proud heart rejoices in itself.
Look at the first words of Hannah’s prayer in verse 1: “My heart rejoices in the Lord.” Hannah shows us the proper response to the Lord’s blessing.
Our response could be proud, but instead it should produce humility in us, because we are receiving a gift from God
God distributes and withholds.
We falter when we begin to depend on those things too much.
Praise God when you receive that check.
Remembering that it’s arrogant to assume our plans will always go forth.
Every single one of us has been given an amazing opportunity with each word we speak.
We get to use our words to help others see God for the glorious Lord that He is.
Instead, use them to do what you were created to do—give glory to God.
I’ve got some studying to do to build my convictions on this for sure.
Forgive us for hurting you and people when we speak with pride.
Ever since I’ve have children of my own I see myself in them daily.
I love this: “Talking proudly is when you don’t acknowledge God as the One who’s sovereign and deserving of glory, in your heart or the words themselves.
Even in our humility, we need to be careful that we are not doing to make men see that we are humble, which in itself is prideful.
I don’t mean to throw Trip under the bus or have any intention in starting a debate online.
I am a recent graduate looking for a job.
You’re not throwing me under the bus bro!
As I said above, proud speech is when you use your words to exalt yourself.
You can explain your achievements and convince someone you’d do a good job without robbing God of His glory (after all, our devotion to Him should lead us to work with excellence).
How, in a culture that frowns upon Christian values being expressed, are we suppose to get, and hold an honest paying job, without our pride getting the best of us?
I think the problem is not being able to express our self at a workplace without the fear of being fired.
We praise athletes and sports teams tirelessly, and our entire world operates on awards and platforms that honor excellence.
I guess just by, in our hearts, acknowledging that our excellence and the work of our hands comes from Him, and attributing all accolades we receive to Him.
