There’s not a person in here who’s perfect.
When the Bible talks about sin it means disobeying God with your actions, your words, or your thoughts.
And the Bible says that sins are not just random broken rules, but that sin is disobedience aimed directly at God himself.
In the same way you’d take someone burning your house down, not only as an attack on your house, but on you yourself, God takes any disobedience by his creation as an attack on him.
Here’s my question for you: Do you expect to be forgiven of those sins?
Why would a good judge ever let sinners off the hook for such a heinous crime as sin?
And there’s only one who’s ever claimed to pay for sin, Jesus.
In Your Sins
What it means to be in your sins, is that you have to pay for them.
It means you’re still in bondage to them.
It means you won’t be forgiven for them.
The cross is where Jesus triumphed over sin.
And if Jesus didn’t get up, that means sin won.
There’s no explanation for why a good judge would overlook them.
None of us would commend a judge who let murderers off the hook because they helped an old lady across the street the next day.
You do not want to stand before God in your sins.
And if Jesus didn’t raise, there is no forgiveness.
Jesus made a lot of religious types mad because, not only was he healing people, but he was telling them their sins are forgiven.
The pope doesn’t have that authority, pastors don’t have that authority, priests don’t have that authority— only God.
Let’s make sense of the word forgiveness.
It would not be forgiveness if I said it’s ok, but planned to slap him back for it later.
Now I may be traumatized and duck whenever he goes for a high five.
I won’t try to get back at him, or be bitter towards him.
Sin is a serious offense against God.
He knew it had to be punished.
And Jesus loved us so much that he was willing to take that punishment at the cross for us.
It’s Jesus stepping in and taking punishment that sinners deserve.
He’ll wipe our record clean.
He’ll accept us into relationship with him.
Romans 4:7-8 says, “Blessed are those whose lawless deeds are forgiven, and whose sins are covered; blessed is the man against whom the Lord will not count his sin.”
But there is no greater blessing in all the universe, than to be forgiven by God.
And I think that’s tragic.
It’s admitting we have no righteousness.
So if Christians have misrepresented the faith to you, be gracious with them.
Do you long to be forgiven of your sins?
