“I think He’s busy hold the line please/ Call me crazy I thought maybe He could mind read.”
God already knows what you’re thinking before you tell Him.
Is it just an exercise in discipline?
Everywhere David looked, Saul was hiding snares for him.
He threw a spear at him and tried to pin him to the wall.
He tried to convince his son, David’s BFF, to kill him.
We feel like no matter where we turn there’s trouble.
That’s why we’re distressed.
But he’s saying that when his spirit is faint, when his spirit is feeble, when he’s overwhelmed, it is God who knows his way.
David is separated from his wife and separated from his best friend, Jonathan.
He feels like nobody cares about Him, like nobody can save him.
David is at the end of himself.
He’s in trouble.
But look, you don’t ask for something if you don’t know you’re in need.
David feels that desperate need and it leads him to cry out to God.
David doesn’t ask as if he’s entitled to this help.
And all of our prayers should be the same.
We don’t deserve anything.
Often I ask myself how I can help a friend who’s in a position like this.
The only thing I know to do is help them get to the place where David is.
Help them get to the end of themselves when they recognize their need for Him and trust Him more.
It would be foolish for David to consider running to anyone else, when this situation was clearly out of his, and others control.
There is no enemy who can legitimately stand against Him.
And His track record is incredible.
In fact, the person praying is a much later relative of the man of who wrote this Psalm.
Jesus knows He’s going to the cross.
He wants to be delivered from the pain that was ahead of Him.
God did not deliver Jesus from the pain and unimaginable suffering that was ahead of Him.
He knew this was what He came to do.
And when Jesus hung on that cross, He was suffering so that you and me wouldn’t have to.
He lived a perfect life, He hadn’t offended God.
We don’t have to win this battle, we just cling to the One who already has.
The truth is, if you haven’t truly trusted in Christ, God is not your friend in times of trouble.
He has not promised to be your treasure or your deliverer.
Here he makes clear why he wants to be delivered, why he wants to be set free – because He wants to praise God as His deliverer.
