]]> What do winners look like?
We don’t need coping mechanisms, false gods, and self-soothing methods.
Because even in the midst of troubles we can find everything we need in God.
What does it really mean?
So how does God meet all of our needs?
We saw it in the Psalm from last week and we’ll see it again this week in the very next Psalm, chapter 142.
And what we’ll see in this prayer of David is a troubled, helpless, desperate man crying out for help.
We see the story of David’s life in the book of 1 Samuel.
He’s probably most famous for his scrappy defeat of Goliath.
When David did that, Saul was still the king.
They even sung songs about how amazing he was, comparing him to Saul.
So even though he had King Saul’s blessing before he killed Goliath, Saul now looked at David as his enemy.
So when David writes this Psalm, he’s on the run.
The king has made the death of David number one on his list of priorities.
And so here in Psalm 142 we find David, hiding out in a cave.
Set me free from my prison, that I may praise your name.
And we’re going to think about four specific things we learn about God in this prayer from David.
The first thing we see is that God is our friend in times of trouble.
It’s actually pretty remarkable when you think about it.
Skeptics may think that God caring about our troubles doesn’t really matter or make much of a difference.
But they’d be very wrong.
Whether or not someone cares about your situation changes how they respond when you tell them about it.
Well I can call and tell them all of my issues with my cable.
But because I called the wrong number, they don’t care.
Actually I’m pretty sure they don’t care even when I call the right number, and they never do anything about it, but you get what I’m saying.
Now I want you to imagine what it would be like if God didn’t care about our troubles.
Ask David if it matters that God cares.
I cry aloud to the Lord; I lift up my voice to the Lord for mercy.
This is a man in urgent danger.
It’s like he’s in a burning building.
And as David prays, he understands that God is his friend and He cares.
He says, “Listen to my cry for I am in desperate need.” Now I don’t think this is him trying to convince God to switch it up and listen for a change.
I think David is saying, “You’ve promised to listen to your people when we talk to you.
I’m talking.” It’s like he was tapping him on the shoulder.
I have something to say.”
He knew that God had made a covenant with this people.
He knew about how God had heard their prayers and delivered them over and over again.
Do you pray with a sense of God’s intended care and love for you?
Is it just a ritual?
He doesn’t put God on trial.
That’s untrue.
Learn from David here.
Look at what he says in verse three.
In the path where I walk men have hidden a snare for me.
Everywhere David looked, Saul was hiding snares for him.
In verse four he keeps telling God about what’s going on.
I have no refuge; no one cares for my life.
What a difference that makes when friends have turned their backs on you.
And co-workers don’t care about you.
God knows you and God cares for you.
God knows how many hairs are on your head.
And in this sense, the believer in Jesus is never alone.
We want them to care about us.
And He loves you.
Truthfully, without God as your friend you would be abandoned.
And many of the world’s laments do end there.
And He knows that when He needs someone to care for him, he should run to God.
But surely God as our friend isn’t all we need.
He says, “I have no refuge.” Now when we hear the word refuge, we automatically go to this metaphorical sense.
That’s not what he means here.
I have no shelter.
Which is why he’s hiding out in a cave.
Can God be a house for us?
I don’t have anyone to listen, but you’ll listen to me.
Now he’s saying, I don’t have any shelter, but you’re my shelter.
While I was working on this sermon I really zoomed in on this verse.
What does this really mean in real life?
And we see it all over the Scriptures.
But he is saying in the midst of his lack of physical shelter, God is another kind of shelter for him.
What does it mean for God to be your refuge?
Though we’ve made our homes into our own little heavens on earth with comforts and entertainments of all sorts, protection is essentially the purpose of shelter.
Listen to what David says in another Psalm he wrote while he was in the cave.
At the deepest part of who he is, at his core, he finds his refuge in God.
Is it your money?
Better yet, where do you run in times of trouble?
Is it in that relationship or that job?
What a strange place to be, yet this is where every believer in a fallen world should find themselves.
Sorrowful, yet always rejoicing.
What it does mean is that even if the watch does get wet, it won’t be destroyed.
God hasn’t promised to keep us from being in a storm, but He’s promised that we will not be destroyed.
And it says even in the midst of all these things, we’re more than conquerors through Christ who loves us.
So look, as our protection, our God will protect us from everything that will turn out in the end for our harm or destruction.
Because we know if He lets it hit us, it’s for our good and for His glory.
So David’s like, “Who needs physical shelter, when I have God!” God is our greatest need, and when that one need is met, all the others look much smaller.
I’m asking both the Christians here today and to the non-Christian.
Many of us have been running to the wrong things.
And to be honest, that’s foolish.
They will not protect us from destruction.
Then he will say, “Where are their gods , the rock in which they took refuge… Let them rise up and help you; let them be your protection.” (Deuteronomy 32:37)
You’ll just wait until it gets bad and then you’ll hide in God.
You don’t wait until the storm comes to build a house.
You don’t have to panic when it starts drizzling, because you’ve already hidden yourself in Him.
And it continues by running back to Him day after day after day.
Another one of these mass attacks on a school.
That should anger us and grieve us.
Christian, what are you going to say to your friend at work who says, “Where was your God in the midst of this?
Why did He let that happen?” What do you say to that?
No one wants to hear that.
And He’s good.
We know He’s good, and wise, and perfect.
So all I can do is trust Him, pray for those victims, and remember that no situation is so horrible that we can’t run to God.
He is our friend and our protector, but there’s more.
God is our Treasure in Times of Trouble
David calls God his portion.
So this could refer to your share of an inheritance – your portion of what was left behind.
So in his current situation, David has nothing.
So he’s saying, “I don’t have a portion, an inheritance, a reward.
You’re my treasure. You are my everything!” God was His everything.
The documentary was about athletes who at one time were millionaires, but found themselves absolutely broke.
They lived as if things, possessions, materials were their treasures.
Their portion was gone.
Who in Philippians says he’s learned to be content, whether he’s rich or he’s poor.
He knew that all he really needed was Christ.
He was willing to throw everything away that he might have Christ.
Don’t you want to get to that point?
Tozer put it in his book The Pursuit of God, “The man who has God for his treasure has all things in One.”
In light of this, our joy cannot be contingent on our bank accounts.
There is no amount of money that can’t be blown or stolen.
He should be our treasure, even in times of trouble.
You may not see Him as your true reward.
And this isn’t a call to begin to neglect all of your other needs.
He’s saying God is all that I have and all that I need right now in this life.
He’s saying right now, God is all I need.
But if you have God, be encouraged.
Even when we feel like we’re lacking, we can find everything we need in Christ.
We like these kinds of movies, because we’re fascinated by larger than life heroes who can save the day when all looks helpless.
David again is telling God how he feels.
He knew that only God could set him free in this particular situation.
Often that’s all we can do.
But calls us to come to him.
He was given over to death, so that we could be delivered.
I didn’t know how, but I knew they had me.
