Our spouses may have problems with being good listeners, but God doesn’t.
Or maybe we just rant about it out loud to ourselves.
Just last week, I was complaining to my wife about something that was just really bothering me and I was concerned about.
I should have been talking to God about it.
Often venting does nothing more than make us madder.
We’re not in control, but He is.
And when we have issues with someone, we go right to the person to take care of it.
Talk to God about your relational issues before you talk to the other person.
This is why we have a prayer of praise every Sunday morning.
We should be coming up with unique ways to sing His praises.
Thank you God.” The heart that is saturated with love for God finds new ways and new reasons to praise God every day.
I can’t think of anything better to tell another person, than the Good News.
Is it a family member, a colleague, a neighbor, a barber, a doctor?
It’s good for us to use our words to build relationships and common ground with our non-Christian friends.
But it’s devastating if we never actually get around to sharing the Gospel with them.
It would be devastating if we had weekly conversations about sports, but never had any conversations about Jesus.
Recently, I had family members die.
Maybe we point them to a good book or cd that talks about the Gospel (ahem, mine).
That should be the overall guiding principle for our words to other people.
Like I said earlier, our words are like hammers.
God has spoken precious words.
One way is encouragement.
It actually caught me and my wife off guard when we first came.
Affirm others.
Affirm your love for them.
Another way is challenging one another.
Sometimes build up looks like rebuke, or correction.
And we need both encouragement and challenging words.
Remind them of God’s sovereignty and His goodness and His promises.
The most mature decision makers are those who don’t make decisions in isolation.
And help them think through how God would have them respond.
On the one hand, as a church, we do a great job of having good spiritual conversations.
And we need to do more.
God is working in your life and we want to hear about it.
We should not think there is a disconnect between the heart and the tongue.
In his experience churches always said come as you are, but never really meant it.
And when people asked if he accepted Christ into his life, he said he wasn’t ready.
The picture that he had been given of Christianity was one where you had to change everything about yourself; you had to stop enjoying everything you’ve been enjoying.
What is Fun?
Fun is: A source of enjoyment, amusement, or pleasure.
If you’re wondering if it can stay the same, it can’t.
The question is: does it help or hurt your fun?
Or does it help you experience enjoyment, amusement, and pleasure in their true sense?
Are Christianity and fun on opposite ends of the spectrum?
Any Christian guy in a movie or TV show is the worst.
So what do I think?
If you really think about it, it’s an odd question to ask.
If being a Christian means following God through faith in Christ, then to rephrase the question it’s asking: Does God want you to have an un-enjoyable life?
He’s the Creator of all life.
What we know from Scripture is that God is a good God.
He made us in His image and He wants us to live that life to the full.
And that’s what we know him for: winning six championship rings, five MVPs, and 14 All Star appearances (not to mention his unchallenged reign in the athletic shoe game).
The kind of winner who doesn’t have all the makings of a champion.
I’ll be honest with you.
I’ve enjoyed a strong marriage and the beginning of two little lives.
But I’ve also been plagued with weakness.
I know, I had never heard of it before either.
In the beginning it really handicapped me— I was only awake for six hours a day, and exhausted for the other eighteen.
I’ve lived the last seven years with a neverending fatigue.
Energy is like the fuel that powers every area of our life.
Instead my tank lingers right above “E,” and I try to make it through.
I tell you all this, not for your sympathy, but to challenge how you think.
I’m a different kind of winner.
My victory happens in spite of my weaknesses, even my sins.
As it is written, “For your sake we are being killed all the day long; we are regarded as sheep to be slaughtered.” No, in all these things we are more than conquerors through him who loved us” (Romans 8:36-37).
Even when we lose, we’re winning.
The small daily battles don’t determine our fate, because the war has already been won.
Don’t Just Look at You
When I stare at me I think I’ve lost, but when I stare at Jesus I’m reminded that I win.
If I only focus on today or these past few years, I’m tempted to think this is how it will always be.
I know God imperfectly now, but I get to know him perfectly later.
We can also be tempted to focus on those who seem to be winning now.
But the team that’s winning in the third quarter isn’t always the team who wins in the end.
Michael Jordan remains one of the greatest and most popular winners we’ve ever seen.
And none of us has what it takes to win the war ourselves; only Christ can do that.
This is the truth that was in my heart when I wrote these words in “Sweet Victory”:
I mean that day when the gray skies fade out, then I’m winning ’cause I reign with him
Is Jesus just another character in the story?
So we’re going to look at a story and try to answer this question.
When evening came they got on a boat and sailed away.
They came to the other side of the sea, to the country of the Gerasenes.
He lived among the tombs.
Night and day among the tombs and on the mountains he was always crying out and cutting himself with stones.
The herdsmen fled and told it in the city and in the country.
And they came to Jesus and saw the demon-possessed man, the one who had had the legion, sitting there, clothed and in his right mind…, and they were afraid.
And they began to beg Jesus to depart from their region.
And he did not permit him but said to him, “Go home to your friends and tell them how much the Lord has done for you, and how he has had mercy on you.” And he went away and began to proclaim in the Decapolis how much Jesus had done for him, and everyone marveled.
I. Jesus Encounters the Demon Possessed Man (5:1-6)
Unclean spirit is basically just another way of saying demon.
He prefers the company of the dead, to the company of the living.
He just broke them.
He was physically harming himself, beating himself, cutting himself.
It’s kind of hard for us to imagine this kind of demonic torment in our day.
And secondly, because most of us haven’t seen demonic torment in person.
What would we do if we saw this man?
I know me.
He needs help.
They tried to chain him up, but it didn’t work.
There are people like this in our day, who are literally tormented like him.
We classify it as mere insanity or depression, but I’m sure in many of this cases, not all, but many of these cases it is demonic oppression.
We just have more advanced ways to do that now.
The cause for his craziness is more than just some trauma that altered his state of mind.
He doesn’t like living like this.
Do not be deceived brothers and sisters.
They’re spiritual beings.
In the Gospel of Mark, Jesus has a lot of run-ins with demons.
And most of the time in the Gospels demons are oppressing people and attempting to destroy God’s creation.
They are trying to destroy the image of God in Him.
They tempt us to sin.
Of course we’re still responsible where we yield to their influence.
He’s been in the tombs as far away from people as possible.
This passage gets a little tricky here because the demons speak through this man, but I think the text leads us to believe that the man was in control when he ran to Jesus.
Maybe Jesus can free him from the oppression.
We can learn something from the demon-possessed man here.
Some of us need to come to this same point of brokenness, where all we can do is run to Jesus.
Jesus calls, “Come to me all you who are weary and heavy laden, and I will give you rest for your souls.” We don’t know how we’ll be delivered, but we know who can do it.
More than anything else, they need to be delivered.
As soon as the man falls before Jesus he cries out, “What have you to do with me Jesus, Son of the Most High God?
Now we’ve already established that Jesus and the demons are not on the same side.
And let’s say a foreign country has some POWs and the US troops come in to save them and the prison guards say, “Ah US Army?
The demons don’t have to ask who this is standing before them.
They say He is Jesus, the Son of the Most High God.
This actually sounds a lot like Peter’s confession in Matthew 16 that Jesus is “the Christ, the son of the living God.” The demons actually just proclaimed the truth of God.
Which I think shows us a frightening truth.
You might come to Summit Church every weekend.
You might listen to every podcast and read every theology book.
Knowing Him has to do with deep, intimate relationship – interacting and loving and trusting at a deep level.
Memorizing facts is not what God has called us to do.
You can say true things?
They know He will oppose them
Basically, why are you here?
They know that Jesus, the Son of the Most High God has come to bring the Kingdom of God.
They know that includes their destruction.
In Rome a Legion was five to six thousand soldiers.
People usually know when they have absolutely no chance to win.
They know that He is their authority
You don’t ask for permission from your equal.
They can’t go anywhere or do anything without the permission of Jesus.
If they had it their way they would torment all of us like they did this man.
These demons were made through and for Jesus.
The power of the word of Christ is really on display here.
Jesus is God and when He speaks and commands it must be done.
The Word of Jesus is more powerful than the heaviest artillery.
The story after this He shows His Lordship over sickness.
The Bible teaches that there is absolutely nothing exists that is not under the authority of Jesus Christ.
In a moment everything changed.
Jesus has come into the world to restore His creation that is so marred by sin and death.
And He shows mercy to this man and delivers Him.
He doesn’t wait for you to earn it before He acts in mercy.
Jesus can deliver us from absolutely anything: sin, addiction, depression, pain, abuse, bad marriage, whatever it may be.
Many of them witnessed the whole event, and the others heard about it.
Maybe they’re angry that their pigs ran off the cliff.
He disturbs their normal pattern of life.
Little comfy Jesus in a box doesn’t exist.
Jesus tells him to stay and tell others.
What He’s delivered you from?
Could be deliverance from a particular sin struggle.
Could be healing from sickness.
Some respond with confusion, others with hostility, some with just mere applause.
He disarmed the rulers and authorities and put them to open shame, by triumphing over them in him.
Though Jesus won many battles against the enemy during His time on earth, His ultimate defeat of evil happens at the end of Mark’s Gospel.
It appeared as if He had been defeated for a moment.
At the cross Jesus put these demonic forces, or rulers and authorities as Paul calls them in Colossians, to open shame.
Almost like the republican primaries.
These demons continue to rebel against God, though they’ve clearly already been defeated.
He is the Lord of all.
This is the kind of Lord that I want to follow.
It is quite possible that we can go through difficult things that only weaken us and bring us closer to death.
Optimism can only survive so many beatings until it breaks, and reality finally chokes it out.
But is the well-meaning sentiment Hitchens attacked ever true?
When Is It True?
If this life, this world, and this body are all there is, Hitchens is right.
It would be like saying, “What doesn’t total my car makes it stronger.” That’s ridiculous.
This doesn’t mean that Christians are invincible.
They make us depend on Jesus
It never fails.
I become proud because I think I’m the reason everything’s going well.
Paul recognizes that God gave him a trial to “keep from becoming conceited.” And he says he boasts gladly in his weakness, and that he’s content with all kinds of trials.
I think Paul is saying that these trials make clear that He needs God’s grace and power.
They make us more like Jesus
Comfort and health are good things that God delights to give us.
And He’s even willing to use trials to accomplish that.
James 1:2 comes to mind, where he tells us to “Count it all joy, my brothers, when you meet trials of various kinds…” The reason we should count it joy is the Christlike character it produces.
They make us long to be with Jesus
But when the temporary joys of this world are taken from us, we’re reminded that Earth is not our home.
I love staying in nice hotels, and when I do I try to take advantage of all the perks.
It reminds me that this hotel isn’t my home.
This earth isn’t where we belong, and trials remind us that our paradise is elsewhere, with our Lord.
Not through positive thinking and optimism, but through real hope and real change.
