]]> People have asked Trip what his new book is about.
The only time I actually made it onto the floor was at the very end of the game—you know, when they put in the guys they feel bad for.
As we shook hands with the opposing team after the final buzzer, I felt like the players were snickering inside when they looked at me and said, “Good game.”
None of the middle school kids looked up to me.
I was a fan wearing a jersey.
Those guys are still professionals, but if you wish you could trade places with a basketball player, it’s usually the starter, the guy who is out there from tip-off to final buzzer.
Those are the guys you want to be.
But for some reason we do the opposite when it comes to life.
You’d hear more about thoughtless fun than playing a role in the grand story.
I’m willing to assume you’d receive similar responses at a high school or an office building.
I hope you don’t take what I’m saying as judgmental condemnation.
I’d be lying if I said following Jesus always seems appealing.
I remember a time, soon after I joined the staff at my church, when all my friends were going to perform at an incredible music festival.
I had to fight the envy in my heart.
Sometimes we feel that way with life.
But even when we know people are living wrong, we sometimes find ourselves longing to join them.
But why do we envy others and assume we’re missing out on something?
It’s one of the consequences of our sinful hearts.
It’s part of what it means to be young in a fallen world.
It’s hard to imagine the whole puzzle when you’ve only seen three pieces.
Have you ever tried driving in a snowstorm?
The snow overpowered the headlights on our SUV and fell so fast that the windshield wipers couldn’t keep up with it.
I didn’t know what was ahead, and I just had to hope it would all work out.
Hitting your brakes meant your car slid like a kid wearing socks on a tile floor.
If we only make decisions based on what’s right in front of us, we’re bound to run into danger.
Getting drunk at a party may seem like a good idea in the moment, but it dishonors God and leads to stupid decisions.
We’ll dive in to these types of decisions more as we go through the book, but the point is, we have to think big picture.
And without looking at the big picture for reference, we’ll place the pieces incorrectly every time.
*This is an excerpt from the third chapter of Trip’s new book,Rise.
