]]> The way we respond to a message depends on who it’s from.
And I think those five words, “All Scripture is God-breathed,” help us fight some of the lies that we sometimes believe about the Bible.
Many of us treat the Bible like a book full of suggestions or a collection of wise sayings.
God doesn’t have “opinions.” He’s God.
Many people today talk about the Bible as an old book with old perspectives that we need to get past.
Others say, “It’s old – get what you can from it, but don’t take it too seriously.”
God is not a man who’s influenced by some cultural setting.
The Bible is old and it was written in different cultures, but all of the truth is just as true today.
Because Scripture is God-breathed it’s not: Just Information
We go to Scripture so we can learn stuff and feel deep.
It is those things, but never just those things.
Never read God’s Word without that reality at the forefront of your mind.
We don’t want to read the Bible because it doesn’t really grab us.
I’ll just say one thing: How could it be boring to see God!?
When He spoke demons cowered, winds and waves retreated, diseases fled, and dead people came back to life.
It’s by God’s Word that any of us can be saved.
In my last post, I argued that we should use our words to boast in God instead of ourselves.
But I don’t think our bragging should be limited to our words.
Jesus tells his listeners to live bright lives, full of good works before others.
What if we gave generously to help meet the needs of others?
It communicates that we’re not the center of the Universe and that there’s something grand to live our lives for.
Every decision you make is an opportunity to be a mirror, reflecting the goodness of your Maker.
If we only brag with our lives, people may assume we’re just nice people.
And if our words say God is amazing, but our lives say otherwise, our claims lose credibility.
Your clever bumper sticker and Christian tattoo aren’t enough.
What do your interactions with others say?
I know I have to repent daily of failing to brag on Him as I should.
Is there anything in the Universe more valuable than casual sex, or a job promotion, or a pair of Jordans?
Make sure your life makes that clear.
Now there’s no doubt that evangelism is scary and hard and stressful.
What about how others feel about it?
We open our mouths and nobody wants to hear it.
People say we’re behind the times.
We live in a dark, hostile world.
Are we preaching the Gospel so we can force everybody to be like us?
Is it because we all need some kind of hope to cling to and Jesus will do?
If you walk away with one thing let it be that.
From seventh grade boys asking a girl to a dance, to not making in to that college you applied to, to not getting that job you so desperately wanted, rejection is hard.
That’s why we as believers in Jesus, because we want people to see the glory of God, that they would trust Christ, and glorify God with their lives, preach the Gospel.
A. Preach Christ!
The Universe is like a display case for God’s glory.
Trees were formed to flaunt His beauty.
Rappers recklessly run off at the mouth, whether they know what they’re talking about or not.
There are some whose rhymes reflect a sincere – though sometimes misguided – search for and struggle with truth.
But sadly, the Bible, the church, and God Himself are often treated like insignificant characters in a fiction story.
As a follower of Jesus, I am personally offended by what I hear, but more than that I’m afraid for the ones who spew these blasphemous words.
I could begin a campaign to boycott their music.
I’ll let you decide whether either of those approaches are right or wrong.
The music these rappers release is a reflection of their hearts.
Have you ever tripped while holding a glass of water or juice?
As we all know, our clumsiness doesn’t produce the liquid, it just shows us what’s in the glass.
When we speak, or rap, the content of our hearts is spilling out.
If they did, they would never speak of Him this way.
But, they don’t truly understand Him.
Nobody would disrespect Him by associating Him with lewd sexual acts or violence if they understood that He was the eternal Judge of their souls.
When you’ve truly seen Jesus, you understand that He’s incomparable.
Not just in songs and blog posts, but at the dinner table, in the boardroom, and in the classroom.
We live in a culture blinded to the truth (2 Corinthians 4:4).
So if you know Jesus, tell your friends and family the truth.
He’s to be worshipped, loved, obeyed, and enjoyed.
But please, do so with grace.
If you’ve seen Jesus for who He is, it’s not because you’re better than anybody.
He’s indescribably good, and more than worthy of our devotion.
Our eternity hinges on what we believe about Jesus.
Paul is in the middle of an awful circumstance.
We think and we complain to our friends, about how unfair it is, and how hard it is.
But they’re not.
We would wrestle through trials differently, if instead of focusing on how uncomfortable we are we would focus on how to honor Jesus in the midst of it.
Does building your life around Jesus mean that it has to be boring or without joy?
I know the lies may seem more attractive sometimes, but the truth is it’s a dead end.
Following Jesus doesn’t mean we don’t enjoy things, it just means we enjoy them right.
Everything is better when enjoyed in the context it was meant to.
Two forgiven people forgive each other, which makes friendship sweeter.
The joy doesn’t last.
But those who build their life around Christ can never be destroyed.
So we have some details about this good life, but where does it come from?
God wants you to live the good life.
Our sin is what kept us from it.
Like He wants us to be depressed and miss out on all joy.
He loves us so much that even though we sinned and were separated from Him, He sent Jesus in order for us to have it.
There were many differences between the picture of the good life I learned from Paul, and the picture I learned from the culture.
The world sells us a “good life” that we have to earn.
But the biblical picture of “the good life” is different.
For by grace you have been saved through faith.
We get to enjoy Him now in His Word.
There is no greater greatness than the God who created them.
The prayer in this psalm, with its distress and urgency, reminds me of another prayer in the Bible.
Stewardship Some of us are very careful with our money.
Because we know a lot is at stake.
Well that same kind of carefulness, and calculation should be applied to the way we use our words.
And we will answer to God for every one of our words.
Or we can use them carefully build stuff.
Talk to God
