Sin Provokes God’s Anger
God says, “For forty years I was angry with that generation…” Well I think that answers the question pretty clearly.
And it’s the sin of His people that provokes this anger.
But I never really knew what mood he was going to be in.
Before I asked him for something, I kind of tried to feel him out and see what kind of mood he was in.
Your haircut sure looks nice.” And those feeler questions would help me figure out if I should ask him now or later.
So when I asked I could never know for sure if he would respond with joy (“Sure, son!”) or anger (“Get outta my face, son!”).
So God is unpredictable in many ways, but there’s nothing unpredictable about His response to sin.
And in the events alluded to in this Psalm, God once again responds with anger.
Actually, the phrase that’s translated I was angry with that generation, more literally means “I loathed that generation.” That’s what many translations say.
God is disgusted with this people because of how they’ve behaved.
Now I know this may, rub up against some people’s concept of God.
My God doesn’t get angry.
How could a perfectly, holy God so consistently respond with furious anger and even disgust?
God’s anger, though, is not sinful like ours.
As I preach this, I don’t feel the need to tip toe around or apologize for God’s anger.
God’s anger is not a sin.
It is the only holy response to cosmic rebellion and treason from men made in His image.
Our anger is sinful, because we are sinners.
Application: Sometimes when we sin, we’re tempted to think it’s not that bad.
But it’s because all I thought about was that I was breaking some rules.
Well sin is more than just a failure to be moral enough.
And it grieves that person and makes Him angry.
Maybe that does not satisfy you though.
I mean really He was angry for forty years?
Sounds kind of petty, God.
God gives a description of this generation He was angry with.
God says they go astray in their hearts.
So on probably a weekly basis my wife will send me somewhere to get something or drop something off and either I don’t make it without calling her or it takes me five times as long.
Well this is kind of the picture that’s painted here when He says their hearts go astray.
They’re lost.
All sin is more than just external, it happens because our hearts go the wrong directions.
And He also says, “They have not known my ways.” God’s character had been on full display before them – His mercy and His power, etc.
It’s unbelievable that they wouldn’t trust Him after seeing that.
Christian, are you aware that this is what you’re doing when you sin?
You are choosing an alternate path to the one that God has set out for you.
I’m aware of how you want me to live, and I think my way is better.
I’m going to choose my own path.” This is wicked.
Do whatever it takes to see your sin rightly.
Don’t try to make it pretty.
We shouldn’t only think of our sin as “Oh, I lost the battle.
We chose to do something other than what God commanded us to do, because we don’t like what He has to say.
We may go on and say, it is the upbraiding of his providence, the scoff of his promise, the reproach of his wisdom.”
And that warning applies to us as well.
When you stubbornly reject God’s commands, you heart gets harder and harder over time.
We think we’ll never be the kind of person who ends up in unrepentant sin, but we could.
A quick look here, a small lie there, and over time we become desensitized to sin and begin to justify it.
He loves you and has given you commands for your good.
OK, so God’s anger is provoked by sin and sin is choosing an alternate path.
What’s the consequence of choosing this alternate path?
So I declared on oath in my anger, “They shall never enter my rest.”
In this particular incident, God forgives them, but He doesn’t allow them to enter the Promised Land.
So he uses this text to talk about God’s eternal rest.
Actually, in this passage He makes an oath in His anger.
But His anger isn’t fickle like ours.
We should fear this amazing God.
This is for everyone in here this evening.
Those are the only two options. All of us deserve the second option.
We go our own path.
There was a cup, full of furious wrath and anger that God was prepared to pour out on us.
Instead, God has taken that cup from over our heads and He’s moved it.
He died on that cross, but He rose from His grave three days later as our victorious King.
The Good News is that those who put their faith in this Jesus- God has no anger left for us.
He’s grieved at us when we sin, but we will not be punished for them.
And if we continue to trust in Christ, when He returns we can receive Him with joy instead of terror.
But across all those kinds of movies, there are two themes that almost always show up: good and evil.
