I don’t mean like when people go to college and re-create themselves with new nicknames and personalities; I mean something significant had gone on inside of me.
I felt so good that I had to announce it to anyone who would listen, especially my closest friends and family.
I was talking with an older man whom I greatly respected, thrilled to explain my game plan for honoring God with my lifestyle, specifically my sexual purity.
As the words leapt from my tongue at an unnatural pace, I got the sense that he wasn’t as excited as I was.
Why are you trying so hard to do everything right?
Just enjoy yourself, learn from those mistakes, and get serious when you get older.”
Some might have taken that advice as liberating and honest, but that’s not how it felt to me.
I didn’t know much at the time, but I knew I couldn’t just sit around and wait.
It’s not that I don’t appreciate a new day with new opportunities, but getting out of bed just never seems appealing.
When it’s eleven at night, getting in bed is just an ordinary part of my day.
If you look at my iPhone, you’ll see that, sadly, I have about sixteen alarms set in fifteen-minute increments starting just before 7:00 a.m. Why?
Those next three—numbers fourteen, fifteen, and sixteen—are my only chance to actually have a day.
This was at its worst during my college years, when climbing out of bed in the morning felt like climbing Mount Everest without any legs.
The lie I told myself was that staying in bed would be good for me.
Businesses would fold, schools would suffer, the government would be even more chaotic than it already is.
The truth is, no matter what time you decide to rise from your slumber, you only have twenty-four hours to work with.
We sometimes call it procrastination.
(I’ll avoid offending anybody and let you define old.) According to many, youth isn’t the time for great responsibility or expectation.
I had a conversation with a waiter in Phoenix one night not too long ago.
He was really friendly from the moment I sat down, and we ended up having a good conversation.
How long have you worked here?
As he responded to my questions, it was clear that he’d bought into the 7:00 a.m. logic.
Before that he was in Nevada, before that California, and before that he lived on the East Coast.
But when I asked him why he moved around so much, here’s what he told me: “Just because.
Who knows, maybe I’ll find myself.”
Of course there’s nothing wrong with moving a lot or self-discovery, but is there a season in our early twenties—or even our teens—when life doesn’t really matter?
CAN YOU TRUST IT?
The lyric went, “We’re happy, free, confused, and lonely at the same time.”
But I’m willing to take the risk and examine what she says in her song “22.” I know it’s just a fun song, so I don’t want to overanalyze it.
The song celebrates the kind of carefree, light, and easy young adulthood that many of us dreamed about.
Who has time for life when you feel twenty-two?
But can it be trusted?
If you were only created for self-satisfaction and enjoyment, then putting off real life until later may be the best choice.
You can wait until later to wake up if you want to.
*This is an excerpt from the first chapter of Trip’s new book, Rise.
Well let me give you three reasons I decided to get married when I did.
She was beautiful, but more importantly she loved Jesus.
We ended up being part of the same church, so I got to watch her serve, grow, and faithfully give her time to others.
My friends, mentors, and pastors all agreed – she was a godly woman.
By this time I began to think about the reality of marriage and what it means to commit yourself to someone for a lifetime.
I searched through the Scriptures and thought about the responsibilities of a husband, and talked to married friends and mentors.
At first I felt like I had to prove to the naysayers that getting married young is ok sometimes.
Flee from it!” or, “Put marriage off until you can’t put it off any longer,” or, “Only get married when you’re too old to enjoy life,” or, “Play the field for a while and then pick the best one.”
So when people say, “What possessed you to get married so young?” my new response is, “You’re asking the wrong question.” I think at the root of that question is the assumption that marriage steals something away from me, as if my youth is being wasted in committed sacrificial love when it could be used for casual pleasure and flakiness.
It is a means of grace.
I don’t agree with the logic that says, put off God’s richest blessings as long as you can.
This is not to say that everyone should get married when I did.
Some of us do need to mature more, and others of us need to pray for contentment and trust the Lord and His timing.
I just want to dispel the myth that we should delay adulthood and only consider marriage once we’re thirty or older.
Last night on twitter someone asked me if I regret getting married so young.
I’m happier and holier than I was two and a half years ago, and that’s a good thing.
I know plenty of folks who got married just as young or younger than me.
Me and my wife may be young now, but I’m praying for grace to love my wife well until the end of our lives.
You cannot know what love is until you know who God is, because God is Love!
In fact, Isaac layed on on Rebecca and knew she was the one for him.
Speaking from my own experience, I did date others and found what love was not.
The problem was, they were not relationships based on the Word or God’s Love and that is why they did not work out.
No disrespect intended, but I believe that is a largely wrong blanket statement that could really steer young hearts in the wrong direction.
There’s some really good stuff in there.)
And whom you call God comes from a culmination of what you’re taught and your experiences.
And yeah, the statement may be a bit general, but it should be taken with a grain of salt, as in anything.
Whereas, we have made the decision to live our lives in a Godly marriage, through God’s word.
Praying for ALL marriages!
