As we walked through every…
And the main problem with the picture I got of this good life was that it centered around me.
What I was listening to and eating was a bunch of lies.
Don’t get me wrong though.
Those sinful lies were.
I could’ve hated hip-hop, but I still would have been fed those exact same lies from somebody else.
But when I met Jesus everything changed.
And so I was on a new quest to figure out how to live life to the full.
I wanted the truth.
We won’t just be living some other option; we won’t be living the way we were created to.
We’ll miss the big picture.
We won’t live like we were created to live.
Of course Scripture never uses the term “the good life.” But when we say “the good life” we usually mean the best kind of life we can live.
I want us to live the way we were created to live.
In the book that’s how I define the good life.
And let me tell you why.
Christians, Muslims, Hindus, Buddhists, Agnostics, Atheists – all of us live by faith, we live in light of what we believe in any given moment.
I’m really bad with directions.
One time I went and pulled into a driveway to turn around, but then I went back out the same way.
It would be very dumb to trust me to lead you.
Our hearts lead us in the wrong direction.
So instead of allowing our hearts to guide us, we need to live by faith in the truth.
We don’t just wait for him to drop clues from Heaven; we look at what He’s already said.
He gave them instructions for how to live.
We believe the world when they tell us that they have something better to offer than God.
We believe the devil when he says we’re at the center of the universe.
And that’s what keeps us from the good life.
That’s when true life begins.
We have to fight everyday to continue to trust God and believe Him over the lies of the enemy.
I know I’m not the only sinner here tonight.
But that’s not the whole story.
So when we disobey authority, it’s because we believe the lies that authority is there to harm us not help us.
And when we have sex before marriage, it’s because we believe the lie, that that’s the best way to enjoy ourselves.
In Romans 1, Paul says he’s working to bring about the “obedience of faith” – the obedience that flows from faith in Jesus.
How do we feed our faith?
Romans 10 says, “Faith comes through hearing, and hearing through the Word of Christ.”
But every day we’re being bombarded with lies from the world, the flesh, and the devil right?
With truth!
Every day we hear messages about sex, bombarded with impure images, and our sinful hearts desire that and believe those lies.
For this is the will of God, your sanctification: that you abstain from sexual immorality; that each one of you know how to control his own body in holiness and honor, not in the passion of lust like the Gentiles who do not know God.
To continue walking in faith, we need fuel.
If we don’t have that fuel we won’t continue to trust him.
But when I’m not in the Word, I feel it.
And we don’t fight for faith in the Word alone.
For those of us in this room tonight who are believers, what are your relationships like with other Christians?
We need to actually walk with other Christians.
If you’ve put your faith in Christ, you don’t have to give in.
And when you walk in that freedom, you will be able to enjoy life as it was meant to be enjoyed.
So we fight for faith, but what does obedient, faithful life look like?
We’ve heard people say, “Nothing in life is sure but death and taxes.” One popular rapper said, “I’m trying to beat life ‘cause I can’t cheat death.” People understand that death cannot be avoided.
Well in order for me to understand what Paul meant by these four words – “to die is gain” – I had to understand the four that came right before them.
He writes in verse 21, “To live is Christ.” With those words, the apostle told me what life is really all about – not money, not my job, not even family – but Jesus.
They have an obsession with winning.
So they’re not mainly concerned with endorsements, or making the highlight reels, or padding their stats.
They play to win.
They just want to win.
That desire that trumps all other desires.
It’s not that he hates life, or family, or comforts; it’s just that honoring Jesus is supreme.
And that’s clear from what he writes in the passage.
We were created by Jesus and for Jesus.
Jesus is our mediator before the Father.
There is no good life apart from Jesus, because without Jesus life has no meaning.
Your life is not about you, but about Jesus.
You should have big dreams and big goals, but they should be centered on Jesus.
Paul says in Philippians 1 that if he lives on in the flesh it means fruitful labor for Him.
It reminds me of a funny story.
The service is always horrible, but the chicken is so good.
He’s off in the back talking and I get his attention.
Similarly though, we are so easily distracted.
And we’re not passionate about laboring for Him, so all the shiny things the world has to offer pull us away.
We need lawyers, rappers, secretaries, waiters, and store managers that love Jesus.
I know some preachers use passages wrong, to convince us that there are no trials.
I can do all things – what does this really mean?
No, Paul was saying whether I’m rich or poor, I’m content.
God is not like some of our earthly mothers and fathers who won’t accept us if we’re not at the top of the corporate ladder.
To live is Christ is the lens he sees his trial through.
There is no greater greatness than the God who created them.
TO DIE IS GAIN This is why Paul could say “to die is gain,” because whether he died or lived, Jesus would be honored.
We can learn from Paul here.
Do you love Him more than this life?
The only way to get there is by gazing at Him in His Word and turning from the things that cloud your vision.
Turn and run to Him.
Conclusion We can live the way we were created to live.
But by grace God showed me those four words: to live is Christ.
As long as I believed the lies the culture told me, I wasn’t living the good life.
I began to see the good life as a life renewed by Jesus, driven by Jesus, and lived to the glory of Jesus.
But the man who lives for Christ gets a taste of the good life now, and his death only brings Him what He desires most.
