MLK was an incredible fighter and motivator God used.
” Darkness can not drive out darkness, only Light can do that.
I want to start by reading some quotes.
Rappers blaspheme his name and put themselves on a level with Jesus, because they don’t grasp the glory of God.
Young ladies put their trust in relationships because they don’t grasp the glory of God.
People go to hell because they haven’t seen the glory of God in the Gospel and trusted in Christ.
When we get a glimpse of the glory of God in Christ, we’ll never be the same.
We often imagine that Paul is sitting somewhere on the beach with his feet up philosophizing as he writes these letters.
3 “And even if our gospel is veiled, it is veiled to those who are perishing.
5 For what we proclaim is not ourselves, but Jesus Christ as Lord, with ourselves as your servants for Jesus’ sake.
So I want to look at Paul’s example and I want to point out four lessons for ministry in a dark world
Rejection is difficult.
It’s natural for people to be discouraged, and hurt, when they offer somebody themselves or something else and the person turns them down.
The Apostle Paul dealt with plenty of rejection and opposition in his ministry of the Gospel.
You went to Corinth, this pagan idolatrous place, and you preached the Gospel to them.
Yet now, some naysayers have sown seeds of doubt in their minds.
He’s saying he’s not discouraged despite the fact that he’s faced such opposition.
He hasn’t retired and decided to become a magician instead.
Considering everything Paul went through.
Most of us would are tempted to be discouraged, and give up.
So when I read Paul’s words saying he doesn’t lose heart.
I want to know how he does it.
Paul does not lose heart because of the greatness of the ministry that God has given Him.
He’s grateful He was chosen by God’s mercy to serve in this way.
In fact, he points out that this new covenant ministry is even more glorious than the old covenant ministry of Moses.
Our confidence in the Gospel is not based on how the people in our neighborhoods respond to it.
It’s unbelievable that the people in this room take part in a ministry more glorious than the ministry of Moses himself.
Because not only do see their not good enough, but we get to tell them about the One who is.
Christians, we should not lose heart.
I only mean that we shouldn’t be dejected and throw in the towel.
Paul may have been accused of being dirty and self-seeking.
So Paul defends himself, saying, “No, we’ve renounced that.
It sounds a lot like what he said in 2 Corinthians 2:17, “Unlike so many, we do not peddle the word of God for profit.
Paul understands that Gospel ministry demands that we live our lives in a certain way.
Paul then says, “We refuse to practice cunning or to tamper with God’s word.” Or as the NIV puts it, “We do not use deception, nor do we distort the word of God.”
He’s still defending the way he operates.
You don’t have to be a pastor or a TV preacher to distort the Word.
Promising people things Jesus didn’t promise them?
Have you ever been at a banquet or something and you sit down at the table and take a sip of the iced tea and it’s unsweetened?
So they add to it.
They just want to mix something else in there with it, to make people like it more.
“But by the open statement of the truth we would commend ourselves to everyone’s conscience in the sight of God.”
State the truth plainly and openly.
Adding to the truth produces veils; it doesn’t remove them.
It doesn’t make her more attractive; it hides her true beauty.
We should not live in a way that contradicts the message, and we should not change the message to try to make people like it.
But it’s not so easy.
We need to be aware of how Satan works.
Some people go to the extreme of thinking that Satan is basically all powerful.
Then there are some who go to another extreme.
In their case the god of this world has blinded the minds of the unbelievers, to keep them from seeing the light of the gospel of the glory of Christ, who is the image of God.”
Some Don’t See the Gospel
Paul’s response is, if the message is hidden from some, it’s hidden from those that are perishing.
Paul is saying the people who can’t see the truthfulness of the message are the same people who are perishing.
There aren’t some people somewhere with spiritual sight, who live by faith in God’s promises, who will inherit eternal life, who also think the Gospel is stupid.
He will punish those who do not know God and do not obey the gospel of our Lord Jesus.
This ministry is a life or death ministry.
But why can’t these people see?
Paul calls him the “god of this world.” So it’s clear that he has some power, but not all power.
Now as we know Satan HATES us.
What is God’s glory?
Look, Satan’s main goal is not to trick you into joining the Illuminati with a rap album.
And that’s what we want men to see.
He wants us to be enamored with the glories of this world.
Have you been allowing Satan to blind you and deter you from looking at the glory of God?
If so, you are playing into his hand and doing exactly what he wants us to do.
Some of us wonder why our world is so dark right now.
Maybe you’ve been sharing with a friend or a family member for years with no progress and you’re tired.
We shouldn’t get frustrated and give up on people.
If God can save me, He can save anyone.
The fact that blind men can’t see how beautiful the Mona Lisa is, doesn’t mean it’s not beautiful.
C. Jesus Is The Image of God
Jesus is not just a prophet of God.
He is not just the Son of God.
If you want to know what God’s love is like, look at Jesus.
Look at Jesus.
In the visible creation we see God’s works, but in Christ Jesus we have God himself, Emmanuel, “God with us.” The glory of God in the face of Jesus Christ is most sweetly conspicuous, because you are conscious that not only are God’s attributes there, but God himself is there.
No one has ever seen God, but God the One and Only, who is at the Father’s side, has made him known.
So when men see the light of the gospel of the glory of Christ, they see God in His glory.
We preach the Gospel so that men can see glory.
So Satan has blinded men, what are we supposed to do?
Paul is saying, people reject my message because they can’t see Christ.
As a minister of the Gospel, you have one primary task.
We get bogged down, and confused, and discouraged, because we get away from the main thing.
When you come into contact with somebody who’s lost, without hope, without God and you ask yourself what you should do.
That’s what they need.
Now that doesn’t mean don’t help people live their lives well, it just means Christ has to be at the center of that.
What does it mean to preach Christ?
And those who turn from sin and trust in Christ will be saved.
You cannot save others.
You cannot change anyone’s heart.
Don’t try to save them, tell them about the One who can.
Because this is how God has chosen people, when they hear the Good News.
Just this past week, I heard two testimonies from believers in my church.
One was a drug dealer who was locked up.
And you know what finally moved them to trust Christ?
We never know when God is going to work, or how God is going to work.
Some of us like to share our testimonies, which is good.
We are doing the very thing Paul says He does NOT do in this passage.
In what ways have you been sharing the Gospel lately?
What do you talk about when you sit in the barber chair, or when you get your hair done?
He’s saying I proclaim Christ as Lord, and me as your slave, for Jesus’ sake.
For somebody to hear the Gospel from me, does it have to be the perfect situation?
We work a lot, or we serve at church a lot, and we don’t ever spend time with non-Christians.
But we shouldn’t leave telling people about Christ up to chance.
There are people who don’t know.
Where are the urban missionaries willing to go elsewhere?
But what’s the point of preaching Christ if men are blind?
Hope In The Work of God
We can’t open blind eyes.
Is God asking us to do the impossible?
In Acts 26:18, Jesus says to Paul, “I am sending you to them, to open their eyes and turn them from darkness to light, and from the power of Satan to God, so that they may receive forgiveness of sins and a place among those who are sanctified by faith in me.”
Man can’t do that.
He wants to give us a quick reminder of His track record.
This is not a God who was born yesterday or even a million years ago.
This is not a God whose hands are tied by the so-called god of this world.
Wicked minds are no match for our God.
He said, “Let there be light” once and He can say it again.
The imagery he uses is like this.
No doors for light to creep under.
Evangelism is when someone stands outside of that dark room and screams Christ is Lord.
And then another time the same News is shared, but this time the wrecking ball of God’s mercy smashes a whole through one of those walls and the brightest light you’ve ever seen shines through.
It’s not ultimately the doing of the person who shared with you.
Only God can do that.
C. Looking At Jesus Regularly With Gratitude
A lot of us, including myself, are not passionate enough about evangelism, because we’re not grateful enough that we get to gaze at the glory of God in the Gospel.
We tell them about shows we like, and good albums, and conferences.
Soak yourself in that Gospel, but God is still showing His glory off there, in the face of Christ.
Don’t try to move past the Gospel!
God is showing Himself off.
When you’re discouraged, remember who you’re serving.
But we can learn from Paul.
Second, to beware the work of Satan.
I don’t want you to leave her discouraged.
I want you to leave here encouraged, at what God has done for you in Jesus.
There’s too much is at stake for us to walk around feeling bad for ourselves.
There are only two options for us when this life is over.
And later, after He returns, all of us will get new bodies.
That is glorious.
