There are some people who are good at certain things.
They wanted to play the game they loved and they wanted to make a career doing it.
He was great – many argue, the greatest.
Mike wanted to be better than everybody.
So he stayed in the gym later than everyone and he worked harder than everyone.
You saw this at his hall of fame speech.
He was driven.
He wants to be the greatest of all time.
He listens to himself.
He’s probably forgotten more rhymes than most rappers have written.
Normally when people talk about being driven, they mean something like this.
But is there more to being driven for the Christian?
What should be driving the Christian and where should it be driving us?
The first eleven chapters have been about what we should believe, how we’re saved, what God has done in Christ, what God will do in Christ, etc.
Paul does this in Ephesians and Colossians too.
I appeal to you therefore, brothers, by the mercies of God, to present your bodies as a living sacrifice, holy and acceptable to God, which is your spiritual worship.
I appeal to you therefore, brothers, by the mercies of God… (Romans 12:1a)
There is a huge difference between what the world is driven by, and what we should be driven by.
She told me about the business and how hard she worked.
And she said that her life was all about the business she started and her children.
Now that seems like a fine thing to say.
But I want you to know that the Christian is to be motivated by something deeper.
So we should love and provide for our kids and we should work hard and we should even seek to delight in the work we do.
None of those should be the main thing that drives us.
He appeals to us, not by what we can do for others, but by what God has already done for us.
So when Paul says the mercies of God, what might he have in mind?
Because in 1:16, Paul says this Gospel is, “the power of God for salvation, first to the Jew and then to the Greek.”
Or maybe he’s thinking about chapter five where he says, “God shows his love for us in that while we were still sinners Christ died for us.”
By sending his own son…”
I think that Paul has all of these beautiful truths concerning God’s mercy in mind here.
We should be motivated not only by the mercy we’ve already received, but by the mercy we WILL receive.
We are to be motivated, driven by that mercy.
So if the Christian is to be driven by the mercies of God, what should these mercies be driving them to?
Now when Paul tells the Romans to present their bodies as sacrifices, the language he uses is meant to point them back to the Old Testament worship and the sacrificial system.
God is holy.
They couldn’t worship Him like they would the idols and false gods they were worshipping.
They could only approach Him the way He laid out and allowed.
God told them to build a tabernacle and he gave them the sacrificial system.
The slaughter and sacrifice of animals was a huge part of Old Testament worship.
By these sacrifices they acknowledged their sin and need for forgiveness and the sacrifices reminded them of God’s holiness.
The animals died instead of them.
So the place that they could meet with God was the tabernacle (a little tent where God dwelled) and later the temple.
He told them you may worship me, in this way, in this place.
Well the NT makes clear, that these sacrifices were never meant to be permanent.
Similar to the animals, that must be clean, Jesus was the perfect sacrifice for our sins.
It never has to be repeated.
But unlike the sacrificed animals, Jesus rose from the grace three days later, defeating our enemies.
The coming of Jesus changes the worship of God forever.
You worship what you do not know; we worship what we know, for salvation is from the Jews.
The temple was, at this time, the place where God allowed His people to approach Him.
We no longer have to meet with God in a particular place with a particular animal to sacrifice.
With the death of Jesus, those sacrifices are done away with.
The place where we must be to worship God is in Christ.
All you need is Jesus!
I think Paul basically means, we are not to offer part of ourselves, but our whole selves up to God.
We’re to present our life, our being, our all to God.
Some of y’all maybe graduated recently and got graduation gifts.
The whole point of giving somebody a toy is so that they can play with the toy.
These gifts are given as a means to something else.
Now, what if you treated those monetary gifts as an end in themselves?
That would be a waste and the giver would be angry.
Every single gift you get, is meant to be used as a means to another end.
For the sacrifice it had to be a clean animal, the person had to be repentant and willing to obey God, etc.
So how are we to give ourselves?
He’s told us the kind of lifestyle that will be pleasing to Him.
In verses 3-8 he talks about using spiritual gifts for the good of the whole body.
We also see this in Ephesians 4 when Paul gives this same kind of charge.
He starts telling them how they are to live.
And he talks about the body working together to build one another up.
I can’t help but notice that in both Romans and Ephesians, Paul begins by speaking to body life.
To ignore God’s church is to ignore God’s will for your life.
And he goes on and on in chapter 5.
He’s told us “be holy as I am holy.” But so often our lives look the opposite of holy.
“Therefore, if anyone is in Christ, he is a new creation.
What would you do if you saw a baby telling their parent what to do?
Because they are the parent, but they are acting like the child.
God made us brand new creatures.
Many of us still think of ourselves as just sinners.
They are reminding them that that is not who they are anymore.
God is calling you to be like Christ.
Have you ever seen those movies where someone saves another person’s life and after that they just follow them all day every day?
Well our response to God’s mercies should be radical and extreme as well.
But if you redeem me from my sins and promise me unearned eternal life, the only rational response is offering you my whole life.
Our whole lives should be this living sacrifice offered up to God.
We want to succeed; we want to climb the ladder.
But you should make sure you’re grinding properly.
Or are you concerned with worshipping God?
Some of us work extremely hard, and we’re driven by a need for our parents’ approval.
And some of us work so hard just to please ourselves.
We grind for God.
If you are offering up your service primarily to any one other than God, you are guilty of idolatry.
Whatever you do, work heartily, as for the Lord and not for men, knowing that from the Lord you will receive the inheritance as your reward.
Brothers and sisters we have been called out of this world.
He’s talking about the sinful mode of operation that we’re used to seeing all around us.
A good example of this is hot issues like homosexuality.
And the media, and the intellectuals, and the thought leaders are all pushing for the acceptance of homosexuality.
And they will talk about us, ridicule us, and treat us like bigots until we agree.
It is a very strong current.
What about selfishness, or greed, or pride?
And we can’t be conformed to the world.
This is another book where Paul lays out Gospel doctrine, then shifts and tells us how we should live in light of that.
Instead of being conformed to what the world is doing, we should be transformed.
He argues for them to live differently, based on what God has done…
And this is what that transformation looks like.
c. Renewing Your Mind
Paul says clearly, “by the renewing of your mind.”
But our minds do need to be renewed.
I don’t mean like when you say, “I know I’m supposed to do right, but…” No that is sinful thinking that needs to be changed.
And your flesh is only fueled by the world and the devil.
How do we do this?
Everyday we hear lie after lie after lie from our heart, and the world, and the devil.
d. Discerning the Will of God
The will of God and the will of God alone can lead you to living a life that is pleasing to Him.
As you renew your mind, and experience transformed life, you will be able to see the goodness of God’s Word.
I remember the first time the Word hit me.
So there are some of us who are bitter and unforgiving.
There are still some of us who are isolated.
Instead we only build ourselves up.
We must turn from those worldly ways, and be transformed by the renewing of our mind.
Christians we can aspire to be great basketball players, or great politicians, or great rappers.
If you are aiming for earthly things and earthly success, you are aiming to low.
