Sadly, but no need to hide. I am a single mom, full time student, own a small business, currently a staff leader for a church we planted in September 2014 (staff unpaid), and work part time at a safe home for teenage girls who are victims of sex trafficking and yes even after reading what I do I am sure you’re already exhausted!
Call me Harriet I’m trying to get…
The most common are, “Your son is so handsome!” or “What a beautiful family!” But one of the other common responses is, “Is your wife white?” People ask me at shows sometimes too. The answer is yes.
To be honest, I always expected to marry a black woman.
I just wanted to grow in my faith and get a good education.
On top of all of that, I loved being around her.
So I eventually started to ask myself, “Should I marry this girl?”
Jessica didn’t look like I expected my future wife to look, but honestly that didn’t matter to me.
And I was never opposed to marrying a white girl.
But as I grew in my faith and my heart changed, my preferences started changing too.
It feels more like settling to overlook a godly woman merely because of her ethnicity.
There’s nothing necessarily wrong with having preferences, but we have to hold them with an open hand.
Some of our preferences really don’t matter that much.
When you and your spouse are in the middle of conflict, skin tone doesn’t matter.
She’s an amazing mom and a godly influence on my son—neither of which have anything to do with her ethnic background.
The ugly reality of racism was pushed in front of our faces,
After all, none of us were there and we don’t know exactly what happened.
You may not be able to.
All of these incidents are minor and none of them significantly threatened my life.
But when I hear his story, I hear my story.
And my son’s story.
Some people think they have the gift of discovering character just by looking at a person.
No conversation has happened, but an imaginary rap sheet is attached.
They think about the gangster image they saw on TV, or the danger their parents told them about, or the horrible crime they witnessed – and they place all of that baggage on a person they’ve never even met.
These kinds of assumptions are disgusting and false.
The Good News is that Jesus came to die for both racist and non-racist sinners.
Who will step up, address this issue, and proclaim the truth?
Maybe we should move on in one sense.
We don’t know every single detail of that night; otherwise we would have been called as witnesses.
Maybe we should stop arguing, and start praying.
But in another sense, we most definitely should not move on.
If you’ve experienced it, help those who haven’t experienced it to understand.
If you’ve never been on the receiving end of racism, sympathize with those who have.
You can’t love someone if you ignore or belittle their concerns.
