And yeah, the statement may be a bit general, but it should be taken with a grain of salt, as in anything.
And they think this means they will no longer struggle or face temptation.
The question is not whether we will face temptation.
I read a quote this week that said, “All men are tempted.
Has there ever been a man who could resist even the most well placed temptations?
Jesus, full of the Holy Spirit, returned from the Jordan and was led by the Spirit in the desert, where for forty days he was tempted by the devil.
A little earlier, in chapter three, John the Baptist is preaching about Jesus and baptizing others, and Jesus Himself comes to be baptized by him.
The Holy Spirit descends on Him in bodily form like a dove; and a voice comes from the Heavens saying, “You are my beloved Son; with you I am well pleased.” So here we have God declaring Jesus to be more than just a mere prophet, more than just a Messiah; Jesus is the very Son of God.
Jesus is unique.
So, who better to look at to learn how we should respond to temptation?
But thatʼs not where the Holy Spirit leads Him.
The Holy Spirit leads Him to the very place where the devil will tempt Him.
He tells Paul of the danger that is before Him, yet constrains Paul to go that He may preach the Gospel.
The Holy Spirit knows that Jesus will be confronted and challenged by the devil, yet He leads Him there anyway.
In light of Godʼs declaration that Jesus is God-pleasing and the Son of God, and Lukeʼs genealogy that shows Jesus to be the son of Adam as well, this temptation sets the scene for Jesus to prove that He is who He was just proclaimed to be.
Itʼs almost like the way many responded to the Miami Heat this season.
So everyone has listened to their boasting, theyʼve heard all the hype about this super team.
Are you this amazing dream team that youʼve proclaimed yourself to be?
Well in similar fashion, the stage has been set for Jesus to show Himself to be the Son of God.
But then the tempter, the serpent comes to Eve and deceives them both.
We see this over and over throughout the Scriptures.
So will the second Adam be able to do what the first could not do?
The devil does come and tempt Jesus for forty days.
Jesus shows Himself to be the Son of God who came to save us from our sins and who is our sinless high priest.
Now, I donʼt want my point to be misunderstood to mean that God Himself tempted Jesus to do evil.
But it is very clear in the Bible that us being tempted to do evil does not fall outside of Godʼs sovereignty.
There is nobody and nothing in all of creation that is outside of His jurisdiction.
There is nothing in the world that can happen without the permission of our sovereign God.
God allowed it and intended to use it for His glory through Jesus.
But do know that God is allowing that temptation and He intends to use it for His glory.
But do know that those tempters answer to your Father in Heaven.
Now I know there are many of us who sometimes have narrow ideas of how we glorify God in the world.
I want you to know that this is not the only kind of obedience that matters.
There does not have to be an audience in order for you to glorify the God of the Universe.
In this case God shows Jesus to be the sinless Son of God, who can resist the temptation of the devil.
The text is clear that Jesus has been fasting for 40 days.
There is a difference.
So he was not immune to fatigue, or weakness, or hunger.
And he attacks Jesus specifically in areas that play to his current physical weakness.
He tempts Jesus with things that could be rightfully His, yet Jesus denies Himself.
Brothers and sisters, we should not be foolish enough to think that Satan will not attack us in our times of weakness.
Temptation will remain and wear you down.
And Satan is not going to take it easy on you in times of weakness.
This would be like an army observing weaknesses in their enemies, and deciding to lay off of them.
When armed forces see a weakness, that is the perfect time for them to attack because the possibility of success is higher.
In reality, they will only attack you more.
Itʼs true that the devil never had a chance to lead Jesus into sin, but how did Jesus defeat Him?
Jesus believed the Word of God, over the Word of Satan.
In times of temptation, we shouldnʼt try to resist by mere willpower.
Resisting temptation in times of weakness begins way before the temptation itself.
What are you taking in?
How are you feeding your faith?
But though Jesus had an empty stomach, He was full of the Spirit.
We should have our faces in His Word, where He gives and sustains our faith in HIs promises.
There are times in our own lives when we feel weak for various reasons.
But no matter what the weakness is, itʼs not an excuse to give in.
When youʼre tempted to sexual immorality remind yourself that God said, “You are not your own, you were bought with a price.
Reading this passage we could lean towards being discouraged, thinking, “Man, Jesus is so holy I wish I could be like Him,” or, “Man, Iʼm more like the first Adam than the second.
We are all more like Jonah than Jesus.
Jesus was not just another one of us who happened to be the obedient one.
The obedience of Jesus not only pleases God in that moment, but Jesus obeys for us that we could be pleasing to God.
He passes the tests that Adam could not pass and that Israel could not pass – and that no man before or after Him could pass.
Jesus defeats the enemies none of us could defeat.
Even though Jonah was too bitter to even preach to His enemies, the Lord Jesus came to earth and died for His enemies.
And Jesus rose from the grave, defeating sin, death, and the devil.
And if we would turn from our sins and put our faith in Christ we can receive all the rewards that he earned.
This week when you are tempted to sin, remember that your enemy has already been defeated by your Lord.
Your chains have been broken.
We are indwelled with the very same Spirit and we wield the very same weapon. Conclusion
The writer of Hebrews looks at this temptation and finds great encouragement there:
Let us then approach the throne of grace with confidence, so that we may receive mercy and find grace to help us in our time of need. (Hebrews 4:15-16)
He has been tempted as we are tempted, but He never ever gave in making Him the perfect high priest.
It’s so great to know how much our God knows every temptations we face !
