Because we hear and use so many words every day they don’t mean much to us.
Maybe we think only the really important things we say are significant.
I think our verse tonight communicates something very different.
But I tell you that men will have to give account on the Day of Judgment for every careless word they have spoken.
And so Jesus basically tells them that they’re evil, and can’t say anything good (he has a kind way with words).
And then he drops that bomb on us.
This doesn’t only apply to the Pharisees.
And the following verse is even more frightening.
Why are our words so important?
Like someone caught you on a bad day when you were mad and judged your entire character on that one day?
It’s not like words are just one aspect of our character that don’t define us.
It’s almost like I’m holding a cup, you don’t know what’s in there.
But if I trip and splash or spill some of that drink, you’ll know exactly what’s in there.
When we speak, the content of our hearts is spilling out.
And as Paul Tripp, an author I read this week, said, “You have never spoken a neutral word in your life.”
Every now and then someone will get caught on tape saying something they never would have said in public.
Similarly, we think we won’t be held accountable for certain words we speak.
And when we do our words will stand in as witnesses – either testifying for us or against us.
God will look at the way we spoke to our parents as a child and the way we speak to our spouses or bosses as adults.
God will investigate the jokes we told to our co-workers.
He’ll even scroll through what we typed on Facebook and Twitter.
He says, “every careless word.” The word used for careless is also translated as idle or useless.
Well there are three words, uttered by the Lord Jesus Christ, that supersede every careless word I’ve ever spoken.
And when he hung on that cross, he died even for the sins of our speech.
And for those of us who put our faith in Christ, yes, there are edifying words we’ve spoken that are fruit of our relationship with Christ.
But we would still be condemned by all the other words.
Background In Exodus, after God’s people have been oppressed and enslaved by the Egyptians, they cry out to Him for deliverance.
He reminds them of what He’s done on their behalf.
God promises to bless them if they obey Him, and curse them if they disobey Him.
They’ll be God’s treasured possession, He’ll bless them, and they’ll live happily ever after.
They didn’t keep up their side of the deal.
The book of Jeremiah is primarily a book about judgment.
But there’s a small section (30-33), where our text is found today, in which God reassures them of His commitment to them, despite their rebellion, and prophesies the blessings He will pour out on them.
He tells them of this New Covenant He’s going make with them.
“This is the covenant I will make with the house of Israel after that time,” declares the Lord.
The text says, “This is the covenant I will make with the house of Israel…” Sounds like a national thing right?
Well in Exodus God did make a covenant with His people Israel.
In Matthew 15:24, Jesus says, “I was sent only to the lost sheep of the house of Israel.” But of course the people of Israel reject Him and by the end of the Gospel of Matthew, His plan is clear.
And we see that so clearly in Acts and all of the New Testament, that God’s work of saving sinners has expanded.
Nobody deserves to be saved and restored to God, so He was gracious to redeem people from Israel.
It’s almost like people are dying of thirst, and only one person has water.
But then we find out water is available to all of us.
The main way this illustration falls short is that we deserve to die of thirst.
I think it’s pretty clear I’m not of Jewish descent.
I think it’s safe to say that most of us in here are not Jewish.
That God would set His eye on us as well and that He sent Christ for us.
But now in Christ Jesus you who once were far off have been brought near by the blood of Christ.” (Ephesians 2:11-13 ESV)
He didn’t have to do this, but as we know it was His plan all along.
The story ends with people from all nations and tongues worshipping the lamb together in the New Heaven and New Earth.
So this should impact the way we live together as the body of Christ even now.
Sometimes my church does baptism on Sunday mornings.
They all have different backgrounds, but God’s saving grace was present in all of them.
I pray that when people come among our church, they would marvel at God’s work in the lives of so many different kinds of people.
There is no qualification other than being needy.
A lot of folks I meet don’t know this.
That’s the beautiful mystery of the New Covenant.
We are in covenant with the living God through Christ.
This is also the reason we should send missionaries and pray for them!
Because God has made a new covenant, and He’s included the nations.
So though the text talks about God’s covenant with the house of Israel, we know that his plan was expanded.
“I will put my law in their minds and write it on their hearts.”
I think it’s just using two different words that communicate that God is going do something inside of us.
Now of course the first time God gave them the Law, He wrote it on tablets of stone.
But God is saying, “No, this time around, I’m going to put my law inside of them.”
We associate the heart with relationships and Valentine’s Day and romantic love.
In the Scriptures, the heart is our entire inner man.
The Hebrew word actually means bowels, because that’s the image they used.
The heart is at the core of everything we do.
We were born with certain desires so they must be right.
God ever have a problem with something that feels so right, whether that be premarital sex, or homosexuality, or any moral issues.
When our spouses irritate us it feels right in the moment to disrespect them.
When our income isn’t quite right it feels right to cheat just a little bit on our taxes.
In fact in another place in the book of Jeremiah, it says, “The heart is deceitful above all things, and desperately sick; who can understand it?” (Jeremiah 17:9 ESV)
If God did not commit to working inside of us we would still be hopeless.
We can only work externally because God is working internally in our hearts to desire and to do what He’s called us to.
We should be working out our salvation.
Not that I don’t struggle anymore, but it’s so different.
And then I remember a change where it bothered me.
That can only be the internal work of God.
Because I can rejoice and say, “Hey, He did that in me too!”
Listen to the last part of this verse.
“I will be their God, and they will be my people.”
It’s not just some agreement for Him to do good things for them.
God has not just promised to bless us with things, He’s promised to bless us with Himself.
It’s not like signing a lease for a new apartment.
And there’s a certain kind of ownership there.
It reminds me of the In Christ Alone line, “For I am His and He is mine.
Christian, do you think of God as your God, or as merely the God?
Or is He your Father?
Because the way you view Him changes the way you respond to Him.
Those of us who have trusted in Christ are His people.
I wonder how it would change our prayer lives if we grasped the truth that He is our God and we are His people.
God hears your prayers and He will always respond in a loving way.
Romans 8 reminds us that nothing can separate us from the love of God in Christ.
No matter what kind of trials we’re in the midst of, God will only use them for our good and His glory.
Instead of clinging to the truth that I am His, and He is mine, I start to turn my Christian life into a talent show; where I perform for God and I make into His good graces if I perform well enough.
I don’t have to perform and present myself to Him.
He is our God and we are His people.
I think the most important phrase in this passage is two little words you may have skipped over, “I will.” God says, “I will make a new covenant.” God says, “I will put my law within them.” God says, “I will write it on their hearts.” He says, “I will be their God and they shall be my people.”
It begins and ends with God.
God was the one who sent His Son.
God is the one who raised Him from the dead.
And God is the one who will glorify you.
Even our foolishness can’t make this covenant fail.
If you donʼt know Christ, turn from your sin and believe on Him today.
You may notice, that the picture painted sounds awfully perfect.
When we are with Him we will see a perfect, pure international bride of Christ.
Anger isn’t one those character traits we usually desire.
Anger sometimes even leads to violence.
Is there anything that can make the God of the universe boiling mad?
In Exodus 17, the people of Israel test God by acting as if they can’t trust Him.
So they fight with Moses and tell him to give them water now.
And they actually ask the question, “Is the Lord among us or not?”
Fast forward to Numbers 14, once again, almost like a repetitive broken record, they are once again complaining and saying it would be better to be back in Egypt.
I just had a similar conversation with my three children.
I’m grateful for Trip living out the gospel and teaching others to do the same.
