The Faithful Evangelist by BragOnMyLord For those short on time, here’s a summary of the points: 5 Characteristics of a Faithful Evangelist… 1.
There’s nothing that unique about getting different kinds of people to gather together.
When an artist or a sports team is the center of attention, the people there are brought together by their love for that music or that team.
They don’t even really have to get along.
That isn’t the kind of unity and reconciliation that we’re after.
It doesn’t produce anything.
And it doesn’t really point to the glory of Christ and his Gospel in the same way.
The kind of unity we’re after can’t be produced by mere common musical or cultural interests.
And that of course is why we’re talking about the Gospel and racial reconciliation, not just racial reconciliation.
…he prophesied that Jesus would die for the nation, and not for the nation only, but also to gather into one the children of God who are scattered abroad.
That’s the kind of unity we’re after:people who were many and diverse all becoming one.
We want people to enjoy the fullness there and proclaim it everywhere they go.
But it can only come through the Gospel.
And if you’re not a millennial (aka you’re old), then perhaps you can take note of how to call millennials to this big biblical vision.
There are some challenges to thinking about racial reconciliation and this generation.
Here are three unique obstacles I think we have in calling millennials to this:
Some millennials think racial tension is our grandparents problem.
My grandfather has told me stories that I can not believe,my father protested during the civil rights movement, and my mother tells me stories of remembering being denied hotel rooms as a child.Those aren’t quite my experiences.
One rapper said, “There’s no racism with the Internet.
Yeah, like five generations ago.
It’s the old people that keep on holding on to it…”
There are lots of interracial marriages in our generation.
Yet, we think we’re post-racial, which is a dangerous combination.
But sadly, unless it’s that blatant, we deny its existence.
It just means it’s more sneaky than before.
Social media is an incredible gift.
But my generation can easily fall into the trap of thinking it’s enough to simply share good things on social media.
That’s not enough!
Talking about this online or even amongst our friends is very good, but it’s not enough.
For millennial Christians, we live in a day where the Bible is not respected as it once was.
In Dr. King’s day, people responded to scriptural truth, but not in ours.
They argue that what we really need is a different, more inclusive message.
We don’t need more Christian Gospel proclamation, many suppose.
We definitely need more than preaching, but we can’t abandon preaching the Gospel, or we’ll only have the kind of light momentary unity I talked about above.
So, then what are we to do?
Here are three simple solutions.
And the main thing I want to press home is that only the Gospel produces the kind of unity we’re after.
I know that seems obvious, but it’s too important to be assumed and looked over.
During my time there, I saw the church grow tremendously in diversity, and it was a beautiful thing to see.
It was great to watch baptisms where there was this crazy diversity among the people God was saving.
And I think it happened, not because the church spent most of our time talking about racial reconciliation, but because we spent most of our time talking about the very message of reconciliation.
He didn’t say some kinds of men, but all men.
When the Apostle John uses the word “all,” it doesn’t mean every single person, but all people groups.
God created diverse peoples, and Jesus died to draw diverse peoples to Himself.
The thing that separates us is sin.
And Christ has already dealt sin a death blow for all men.
Otherwise, we’ll waste our time trying to create a different kind of unity rather than embracing the unity Jesus has already created.
I wonder if you’ve been relying on that message in your ministry.
In Ephesians 4, Paul talks about this deep unity.
There is one body and one Spirit—just as you were called to the one hope that belongs to your call—one Lord, one faith, one baptism, one God and Father of all, who is over all and through all and in all.Ephesians 4:4-6
And the Gospel is needed to reconcile us in that deep way.
And it produces true unity.
In the wider culture, we want to see legislation, and we want to see justice.
Only the Gospel can create this kind of unity.
Solution #2: Fight for Gospel love and understanding
As I have loved you, so you must love one another.
But what does this mean, and what does this really look like?
We should have that holy affection for one another.
We should want one another to know Christ more, grow more, and to flourish.
But Jesus doesn’t just say love one another; he specifies how we are to love one another.
And He says we should love each other in a similar way.
This loving one another fleshes itself out in a number of ways.
That’s serious love.
Here’s the thing, if we’re going to love one another, we have to know each other.
How can I bear my brother’s burdens, if I don’t know what his burdens are?
Understanding each other helps us love one another well.
And he said to me, “To me, you’re just my friend Trip.
He wasn’t going to love me any less than any of his other friends.
It would be like me saying to one of my single friends, “You know I just see you as Johnny.
And many of his struggles, desires, and challenges are going to have something to do with the fact that he’s single.
Because it is, and it does affect my life.
Because I am black.
And God made me this way.
There are unique experiences that come with being black, or being a student, or a mom, or being single.
Some of us don’t have to think about our ethnicity much in our daily lives.
We carry the burden of knowing the oppressive history in our country: that blacks weren’t considered full people, that some of the figures on our currency owned black people like property, knowing racism still exists, and being a recipient of that racism sometimes.
It will be hard, which brings me to my last point.
Try not to only flock to people who look like you.
Not as an assignment, but because they are a gift to the body just like those people who are more like you.
Intentionally seek to understand people.
Persevere through difficulties and growing pains.
It’s a two-way street.
Ask yourself if you have assumptions about other races/ages/socioeconomic groups.
We should question ourselves and strive to see people the way God sees them.
Share concerns and struggles, but labor to make sure you have these conversations with compassion, sensitivity, grace, and love.
We need to be aware enough to fight well.
Meditate on scripture and pray God will give you a passion for unity.
And pray God would give you a passion for this kind of unity.
