I wanted to get your take on something because you have been someone who has played a big role in my walk with Christ.
When I became a Christian a few years back, things were simple- trust Jesus completely, God forives you for everything (past, present, future), just trust in him and he will take care of the rest.
Just a kid who trusted in his dad and didn’t worry about needing to be perfect.
I didn’t need to worry about sin because the way I viewed it- my job was to trust Jesus, the Holy Spirit took care of the rest.
My father especially (who was young in the faith at the time, so I don’t hold it against him) was very quick to stress God’s justice without balancing it with teaching about his mercy.
He was huge on speaking in tounges, many other things but didn’t hit much on the trusting God part.
I can’t tell you how many hours I’ve spent researching theological stuff, unanswered questions, looking up scriptures in Greek, etc.
My thought process has been “Well if Jesus has all of these conditions, then I need to make sure that I meet the conditions.” Thus, my walk became intellectual and perfectionistic.
I notice two opposing unbalanced camps within Christianity and I’m sure you may agree- 1) an “obedience” camp and 2) Jesus paid it all” camp.
The Jesus paid it all camp stresses trusting him alone and seems to rely very heavily on Paul.
Camp 1 is great about stressing God’s righteousness and unchanging nature but seems to try to manufacture obedience from fleshly willpower.
Camp 2 is great about stressing God’s forgiveness and the sunshine and rainbows stuff, but portrays God as if he is two completely different people from the OT to NT.
God’s truth has to be somewhere in the balanced middle, right?
I’m having the hardest time getting back to just trusting him and being in relationship with him because I’m constantly worried about “not being able to figure everything out” (I’m relying on my own understanding terribly).
If he has all of these conditions, then my job is to trust that his grace will help me meet them.
There is balance though, and I know you can receive it.
Studying the Word is good, don’t stop.
I’m not talking about praying with a feeling of obligation or a laundry list of requests.
It’s something to practice, I’ve been learning how to do it better myself lately.
It’s about prayer and spiritual warfare and it’s been so great!
I am thinking more and more that in Western society we are spiritually asleep.
You will find peace in prayer – talk with God about your questions.
I hope you’re able to see this and I hope it helps you Evan!
