John is saying that receiving Jesus is trusting in the individual—believing in his name, and everything he revealed himself to be.
In the end, there are two kinds of people when it comes to Jesus: Those who reject him because they love darkness and those who believe in his name.
It’s knowing the facts about who he is and what he’s done, agreeing with those facts, and trusting them—betting your life on them.
You are a brand new creature.
You are a new creature reborn into the family of God.
We’re here to help one another follow Jesus.
In the same way, when God adopts us as his sons and daughters, he treats us like his only begotten son, Jesus.
He has all the benefits without doing any of the work.
Everything the work of Jesus accomplished is transferred to us.
In those times it’s helpful to remember that when he adopted me, he gave me the righteousness of Jesus.
I just need to keep trusting and repenting.
It’s because it’s not good for you.
We are his sons and daughters.
God doesn’t just give us stuff, but he gives us himself.
If you draw near to him he will draw near to you.
