In that process you can choose what you want.
Anyway one of the choices you get to make is for race.
So we chose not to adopt a black child because of them and the way that they would be treated when we took them back to see my grandparents.
Did we do the right thing?
We are comfortable with that choice now, because it is over, but what do you think?
Even the judge who handled the case was black.
Each situation is unique and God gives us a measure of grace to navigate life’s small and large moments.
Who knows, maybe you’ll get the chance to adopt again and choose a black (or other ethnicity) child!
I come from a family where my white parents have adopted 6 black children.
Can I endure all the backlash from friends and family that comes with adopting a child outside my race?
Will I leave space for my child to be able to wrestle through their identity of living between worlds?
How ethical is this adoption process?
Relationships will need to be built with those who are coming from a perspective that you can never fully resonate with (being black).
If you chose to not recognize that, then you are in many ways doing a disservice to yourself and the child you adopt.
It’s about you being motivated out of a place of love and truly living out the heart of the gospel to reconcile and love your neighbor.
In many ways these are just a few questions you need to be confronting.
