Preach Christ, Show Them Glory — TRIP LEE - OFFICIAL SITE
And this letter is written in that context.
Paul still doesn’t lose heart; which is exactly what we would be tempted to do.
He tells us right here in this verse.
The glory of being one of those on this side of the cross, who get to preach salvation by grace through faith in Jesus.
The man who hated the church and persecuted Christians.
Don’t waiver.
He understands that we can’t proclaim the Gospel, and tell people to repent of sin, without living a life of repentance ourselves.
They’re thinking this Gospel must not be as powerful as she says it is, cause she’s a mess or he’s a mess.
Maybe it sounds like an obvious thing, but it’s really not so obvious.
Paul’s saying he won’t do it.
And it reveals how you truly see the Gospel.
Let it loose that the true glory of Christ would shine through.
Both of these extremes are lies and they are dangerous for us to believe.
Paul says these people that are perishing, or as he calls them, unbelievers, they can’t see because Satan blinds them.
Also has a lot to do with His reputation, renown, and presence among His people.
Help them see.
Blind people don’t see.
One of the reasons many of us struggle with evangelism is because we misunderstand what we’ve been called to do.
They aren’t rejecting me, but Him.
But their main need is Jesus.
Paul said to the Corinthians “I determined to know nothing among you except Christ and Him crucified.” Paul means the message about Jesus and what He’s done and how He saves.
Don’t try to clean them up, tell them about the One who can.
Tell people about Jesus, so they can see the glory of Jesus.
And it’s not the only time Paul says something like this.
